    "ls|LS17_S7_U_287": "O.I.H.\n \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA\n \nMINISTRY OF AGRICULTURE \nAND\n \nFARMERS WELFARE\n \nDEPARTMENT OF AGRICULTURE \nAND\n \nFARMERS WELFARE\n \n \nLOK SABHA\n \nUN\nSTARRED QUESTION NO\n.\n287\n \nTO BE ANSWERED ON THE\n \n3\n0\nTH\n \nNOVEMBER\n,\n \n20\n2\n1\n \n \nCoverage of PMKSY\n-\nPDMC\n \n \n287\n.\n \nSHRI \nBALAK NATH\n:\n \n \nWill the Minister of AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE \nकृ \u0000ष\n \nएवं\n \n\u0000कसान\n \nक\u0000याण\n \nमं\u0000ी\n \nbe pleased to state\n:\n \n \n(a)\n \nthe total area covered under Pradhan Mantri Krishi Sinchayee Yojana\n-\nPer \nDrop More Crop (PMKSY\n-\nPDMC) for micro irrigation in the country till date; \n \n \n(b)\n \nthe progress made in the areas coming under micro irrigation during the \nlast three years; \n \n \n(c)\n \nthe amount of funds released to various States as central assistance \nunder the scheme during said period; \n \n \n(d)\n \nthe manner in which State Governments are w\norking with the Central \nGovernment to promote these schemes; and \n \n \n(e)\n \nthe strategy made by the Government for the utilisation of MIF and the \ndetails thereof?\n \n \nANSWER\n \n \nTHE \nMINISTER OF AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE\n \n \nकृ \u0000ष\n \nएवं\n \n\u0000कसान\n \nक\u0000याण\n \nमं\u0000ी\n     \n \n(SHRI\n \nNARENDRA SINGH TOMAR)\n \n \n(a): \n \nThe \nPer Drop More Crop component of Pradhan Mantri Krishi Sinchayee \nYojana (PMKSY\n-\nPDMC\n) \nscheme is operational in the country from 2015\n-\n16 \nwhich \nfocuses on enhancing water use efficiency at farm level through Micro \nIrrigation\n \n(MI)\n. \nTotal \narea of 59.37 lakh ha has been covered under Micro \nIrrigation  in the country \nunder PMKSY\n-\nPDMC\n \nfrom 2015\n-\n16 to till date.\n \n(\nb) & (\nc)\n \nDuring the last three years, \nCentral assistance of \nRs. 8141.96 crore \nwas released to States and an area of 32.6\n8 lakh ha covered under Micro \nIrrigation \nthrough the scheme.\n  \n \nS\ntate wise \nprogress made under \nPMKSY\n-\nPDMC \nduring \nthe \nsaid\n \nperiod \nis Annexed.  \n \n \n \n(d): \n \nThe \nPMKSY\n-\n \nPDMC \nis a Centrally Sponsored Scheme\n \nand t\nhe f\nunds\n \nunder the \nscheme \nis\n \nshared \nbetween\n \nCentral Government and State \nGovernment in the ratio of 60:40 for all \nS\ntates except the Nor\nth Eastern and \nHimalayan states wherein \nsharing\n \npattern\n \nis 90:10. \nIn case of \n \nUnion Territories, \n\nfunding pattern is 100% grant by the Central Government\n. The Central \nAssistance to \nStates under the scheme\n \nis provided \nbased on their\n \nAnnual\n \nAction\n \nPlan \napproved by the State Level Sanctioning Committee (SLSC) \nunder the \nChairmanship of Chief Secretary\n.\n \n \nBesides, \nOperational Guidelines of the \nscheme has been \nis\nsued \nto \nsuppor\nt the \nStates\n \nand farmers on various aspects \nfor\n \neffective implementation\n \nof the scheme\n.  \n \n(e):\n \nWith the objective of facilitating the States in mobilising resources for \nexpanding coverage of micro irrigation, a Micro Irrigation Fund (MIF) \nwith  corpus \nof Rs. 5000 crore was created with National Bank for Agriculture and Rural \nDevelopment (NABARD) during 2018\n-\n19. \nThe \nStates \nare encouraged to access \nMIF for \ninnovative projects \n/ \nadditional incentive to farmers \nfor installation\n \nof \nMicro Irrigat\nion systems\n \nby interest subvention of \n3\n% than\n \nthe cost of funds \nmobilized by NABRAD from the market\n.  \nSo far, p\nrojects with loans under MIF \namounting \nto Rs. 3970.17 Crore have been \napproved \nfor \n12.81\n \nlakh ha\n \nof \nMicro \nIrrigation area\n.\n \n \nTo continue the \nefforts, during the current year, a Budget Announcement \nwas made to double the initial corpus of MIF from Rs. 5,000 crores to Rs. 10,000 \ncrore\n.\n \n\nAnnexure\n \nAnnexure to Lok Sabha Unstarred Question No. 287 for answer on 30.11.2021\n \nCentral Assistance released \nand area covered under Micro Irrigation under \nPMKSY\n-\n \nPDMC \nduring last three years\n \nS. No.\n \nState\n \nCentral Assistance \nreleased\n \n(Rs. in Crore)\n \nArea covered \nunder Micro \nIrrigation (Ha)\n \n1\n \nAndhra Pradesh\n \n1072.00\n \n322349\n \n2\n \nBihar\n \n27.91\n \n6166\n \n3\n \nChhattisgarh\n \n120.54\n \n65251\n \n4\n \nGoa\n \n1.70\n \n417\n \n5\n \nGujarat\n \n825.50\n \n349756\n \n6\n \nHaryana\n \n142.21\n \n45567\n \n7\n \nHimachal Pra.\n \n64.00\n \n5046\n \n8\n \nJharkhand\n \n74.97\n \n11876\n \n9\n \nJ&K\n \n44.80\n \n1090\n \n10\n \nKarnataka\n \n1182.03\n \n806622\n \n11\n \nKerala\n \n9.00\n \n1937\n \n12\n \nMadhya Pradesh\n \n359.56\n \n67509\n \n13\n \nMaharashtra\n \n985.00\n \n380934\n \n14\n \nOdisha\n \n115.00\n \n32171\n \n15\n \nPunjab\n \n9.00\n \n2191\n \n16\n \nRajasthan\n \n443.48\n \n181746\n \n17\n \nTamil Nadu\n \n1278.00\n \n650086\n \n18\n \nTelangana\n \n122.00\n \n56237\n \n19\n \nUttarakhand\n \n125.00\n \n15174.41\n \n20\n \nUttar Pradesh\n \n387.88\n \n170143\n \n21\n \nWest Bengal\n \n121.00\n \n50015\n \n22\n \nArun. Pradesh\n \n55.50\n \n6898\n \n23\n \nAssam\n \n72.00\n \n17674\n \n24\n \nManipur\n \n107.50\n \n8609\n \n25\n \nMeghalaya\n \n27.00\n \n-\n \n26\n \nMizoram\n \n80.80\n \n2867\n \n27\n \nNagaland\n \n118.00\n \n4350\n \n28\n \nSikkim\n \n121.98\n \n5524\n \n29\n \nTripura\n \n46.20\n \n-\n \n30\n \nLadakh\n \n2.40\n \n-\n \n \n \nGrand Total\n \n8141.96\n \n \n3268205\n \n \n****\n*\n \n \n \n \n \n ",
    "ls|LS17_S7_U_288": " \n \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF RURAL DEVELOPMENT \nDEPARTMENT OF LAND RESOURCES \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION No. 288 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 30.11.2021 \n \nWatershed Development Projects \n \n288      SHRI VENKATESH NETHA BORLAKUNTA:  \nSHRI PASUNOORI DAYAKAR:  \n            DR. G. RANJITH REDDY:  \n            SHRIMATI KAVITHA MALOTHU \n \nWill the Minister of Rural Development be pleased to state: \n(a) whether it is true that 330 watershed development projects have been sanctioned for \nTelangana under PM Krishi Sinchayee Yojana, if so, the details thereof;  \n \n(b) whether it is true that more than 200 projects are pending for completion, if so, \ndetails thereof, district-wise;  \n \n(c) the reasons for delay in completing the projects and efforts being made by the \nMinistry to complete the left out projects; and  \n \n(d)  the details of amount so far spent on the above projects, district-wise? \n \nANSWER \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF RURAL DEVELOPMENT \n(SHRI FAGGAN SINGH KULASTE) \n \n(a) to (d) The Department of Land Resources  (DoLR) has sanctioned  330 watershed \ndevelopment projects to Telangana covering an area of about 13.99 lakh  \nhectare during the period 2009 -10 to 2014 -15 under the erstwhile Integrated \nWatershed Management Programme (IWMP). Subsequently IWMP was \namalgamated as a Watershed Development Component of Pradhan  Mantri \nKrishi Sinchayee Yojana (WDC -PMKSY) in 2015 -16. A total number of 54 \nprojects in preparatory stage were transferred to State  in 2018 to implement them \nfrom their own budget. Therefore, DoLR has been financially supporting only 276 \nprojects in State of Telangana.  \n \n\n \n \n \nOut of these, 276 projects, 191 projects have been reported as completed  by State \nof Telangana. Remaining 85 projects are in advanced stage of completion.  \n  \nDue to uncertainty posed by the pandemic COVID -19, State has not been able to \ncomplete planned activities. The State has got Rs. 67.26 crore unspent balance (as \non 06.11.2021) which will be utilized for the completion of these projects by  \nMarch, 2022. DoLR is regularly reviewing the progress of implementation of the \nscheme with  Telangana. D .O. letters to Chief Secretary / Secretary  have been \nwritten. Video Conferences with CEO , State Level Nodal Agency (SLNA) , their \nofficers and f ield visits to State  by Senior Officers of the Department are  also \nundertaken. During these reviews, completion of these projects have always being \nemphasized.  \nAs per information received from State of Telangana, the district-wise details of \nprojects sanctioned, projects completed and funds spent is given at Annexure. \n***** \n \n  \n\n \n \nAnnexure \nAnnexure referred to in reply to part (a) to (d) of Lok Sabha Unstarred Question No. 288 for \nanswer on 30.11.2021 \nDistrict-wise details of number of projects, sanctioned, completed and funds spent for the projects in \nTelangana \n \nSl. No. Districts No of \nProjects \nsanctioned \nNo of \nprojects \ncompleted \nFunds spent \n( Rs. in lakh) \n1 Adilabad 22 10 7572.2 \n2 Bhadradri Kothagudem 19 6 5027.37 \n3 Jangaon 3 0 230.59 \n4 Jogulamba Gadwal 12 9 2022.38 \n5 Khammam 7 1 1092.94 \n6 Kumaram Bheem Asifabad 19 10 6435.89 \n7 Mahabubabad 18 11 366.26 \n8 Mahabubnagar 3 0 4386.94 \n9 Mancherial 11 7 3147.13 \n10 Medak 11 5 3483.71 \n11 Mulugu 7 0 648.91 \n12 Nagarkurnool 30 18 6482.2 \n13 Nalgonda 29 22 8701.69 \n14 Narayanpet 15 8 2669.32 \n15 Nirmal 7 6 2414.09 \n16 Rajanna Sirsilla 1 0 0.09 \n17 Rangareddy 28 19 6826.43 \n18 Sangareddy 20 17 6490.01 \n19 Siddipet 9 3 1520.32 \n20 Suryapet 8 7 2366.91 \n21 Vikarabad 25 17 8974.77 \n22 Wanaparthy 12 9 2582.85 \n23 Hanamkonda 2 0 3.95 \n24 Warangal 1 0 354.3 \n25 Yadadri Bhuvangiri 11 6 1815.97 \nTotal 330* 191 85617.22 \n \n* Includes 4 projects which were transferred to Andhra Pradesh during the process of bifurcation of \nAndhra Pradesh and Telangana..  \n \n \n               ***** \n ",
    "ls|LS17_S7_U_289": " \n \n \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF FOOD PROCESSING INDUSTRIES \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 289 \n ANSWERED ON 30TH NOVEMBER, 2021 \n  \nSETTING -UP OF FRUITS AND VEGETABLE PROCESSING UNITS  \n  \n289. SHRIMATI SUNITA DUGGAL: \n \nWill the Minister of FOOD PROCESSING INDUSTRIES be pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether the Government has implemented various schemes and is extending  financial \nassistance for setting up of fruit and vegetable processing units in Haryana; \n \n(b) if so, the details thereof; and \n \n(c) the details of the fruit and vegetable processing units in Sirsa and Fatehabad district \ncovered/benefited under these schemes during the last three years? \n \n \n ANSWER \n \n \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE FOR FOOD PROCESSING INDUSTRIES  \n(SHRI PRAHLAD SINGH PATEL) \n  \n(a) & (b): The Ministry of Food Processing industries (MoFPI) has been implementing Central \nSector Umbrella Scheme - Pradhan Mantri Kisan SAMPADA Yojana  (PMKSY) since 2016-\n17 for overall growth and development of food processing sector. Under component schemes \nof PMKSY, MoFPI provides mostly credit linked financial assistance (capital subsidy) in the \nform of grants-in-aid to entrepreneurs for setting up of food processing / preservation industries, \nincluding fruit and vegetable processing units. PMKSY is not any region or state specific but \ndemand driven and is implemented across the country including Haryana. So far, Ministry has \napproved 2 Mega food Park s, 20 Cold Chain projects, 2 Agro Processing Clusters & 15 Food \nProcessing Units in Haryana State under corresponding component schemes of PMKSY. \n \n               Also, as part of the Aatmanirbhar Bharat Initiative, MoFPI is implementing a \nCentrally Sponsored Scheme-PM Formalisation of Micro Food Processing Enterprises Scheme \n(PMFME) for providing financial, technical and business support for setting up/upgradation of \n2 lakh micro food processing enterprises across the country during five years from 2020 -21 to \n2024-25 based on One District One Product (ODOP) approach, out of which 4559 units have \nbeen allocated to Haryana. \n  \n(c):  Under the PMFME scheme, Citrus Fruit has been identified as ODOP for Fatehabad \ndistrict and Citrus(Kinnow) for Sirsa district. However, no application from fruit and vegetable \nbased enterprises has yet been received in these districts under PMFME. Also, no fruit and \nvegetable based project has been approved under component schemes of PMKSY in these two \ndistricts, though one dairy based cold chain project has been approved in Sirsa district. \n \n \n***** ",
    "ls|LS17_S7_U_290": "  \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF RURAL DEVELOPMENT \nDEPARTMENT OF RURAL DEVELOPMENT \nLOK  SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 290 \n  ANSWERED ON 30/11/2021 \n \nBUDGET PROVISION UNDER PMGSY \n \n290.  SHRI DHARAMBIR SINGH: \n \nWill the Minister of RURAL DEVELOPMENT be pleased to state: \n \n(a)  the details of the budget provision for reconstruction and repair of roads under \nPradhan Mantri Gram Sadak Yojana (PMGSY) in the parliamentary constituency of \nBhiwani-Mahendergarh from the year 2014-15 to 2021-22, year-wise; \n(b)  the number of roads that have been built during the last five years upto November, \n2021 under PMGSY in the parliamentary constituency of  Bhiwani-Mahendergarh; \nand \n(c)  the number of roads that have been renewed and repaired upto November, 2021 in the \nsaid place including the details of the road-wise development, division-wise? \n \nANSWER \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF RURAL DEVELOPMENT  \n(SADHVI NIRANJAN JYOTI) \n \n(a)     Rural roads and their maintenance is a state subject. Repair and maintenance of rural \nroads are not taken up under Pradhan Mantri Gram Sadak Yojana (PMGSY).  However, \nupgradation of roads has been undertaken under PMGSY. As informed by the State \nGovernment, the details of road works sanctioned from the year 2014 -15 to 2021 -22, year-\nwise, for upgradation of the existing rural roads in Bhiwani -Mahendragarh Parliamentary \nconstituency in the State of Haryana under PMGSY are given below:-  \nYear Name of intervention Road Length Sanctioned \n(in km) \nValue of Project \n( Rs. in lakh) \n2014-15 -- 00 00 \n2015-16 -- 00 00 \n2016-17 -- 00 00 \n2017-18 -- 00 00 \n2018-19 -- 00 00 \n2019-20 -- 00 00 \n2020-21 PMGSY-III 275.63 12,193.46 \n2021-22 -- 00 00 \n \n(b)  12 road works of 105  km have been completed  under PMGSY in Bhiwani - \nMahendragarh Parliamentary constituency of Haryana during the last five years upto \nNovember, 2021, as reported by the State Government. \n(c)     As informed by the State Government of Haryana, t he details of the roads renewed and \nrepaired upto November, 2021 is given at Annexure. \n***** \n\n Annexure \n \nAnnexure referred to in reply to point (c) of Lok Sabha  Unstarred Question No. 290 for \nreply on 30.11.2021 \n \nRoad length renewed upto November, 2021 under PMGSY \nS.No. District Name Work Name Road length \nrenewed ( in Km)  \n1. Bhiwani Tosham Sagwan road 4.35 \n2. Bhiwani Siwani Kikral - Gurera Dhanothi upto \nState Rajshthan \n13.62 \n3. Bhiwani Bhera - Garwa Road 8.96 \n4. Bhiwani PATODI - BEHRA 10.70 \n5. Bhiwani JHUMPA KALAN - ISHERWAL 15.08 \n6. Bhiwani MRL2-Gignow to Sehar via Brahlu 11.50 \n7. Bhiwani MRL3-Behal Sorra road 9.80 \n  \n \n***** ",
    "ls|LS17_S7_U_291": "  \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF RURAL DEVELOPMENT \nDEPARTMENT OF RURAL DEVELOPMENT \nLOK  SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 291 \n  ANSWERED ON 30/11/2021 \n \nMIGRATION PROBLEM AMONG RURAL YOUTH \n \n291.  SHRI B.B.PATIL: \n \nWill the Minister of RURAL DEVELOPMENT be pleased to state: \n \n(a)  whether the Government is aware that the migration problem among rural youth has \nincreased continuously in the country; \n(b)  if so, the details thereof and the reasons therefor; \n(c)  whether the Government has prepared any action plan to prevent the migration of \nrural youth; and \n(d)  if so, the details thereof? \n \nANSWER \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF RURAL DEVELOPMENT  \n(SADHVI NIRANJAN JYOTI) \n(a) & (b): As per National Sample Survey Office (NSSO), NSS 64th Round report, migration \nrates, including those of rural youths, for the period 1983 to 2007-08, from different NSS \nrounds are as under:  \nAll-India (Rural) \nRound(Year) Migration Rates (per 1000 persons) \n64th (July 07-Jun 08) 261 \n55th (July 1999-June 2000) 244 \n49th (Jan-June,1993) 228 \n43rd (July 1987- June 88) 232 \n38th (Jan-Dec,1983) 209 \n  \n(c) & (d):   Migration of workers in search of work from one place to another is a normal \nphenomenon. The problems of migration/migrant workers is sought to be addressed through \nmulti dimensional course of action through rural development, provision of improved \ninfrastructural facilities, equitable dispersal of resources to remove regional disparities, \nemployment generation, land reforms, increased literacy, financial assistance etc. \n   \nSo far as the Ministry of Rural Development is concerned, the Ministry is implementing \nvarious rural development schemes for creating employment opportunities and rural \ninfrastructure. All these measures encourage people to stay in rural areas, earn their \nlivelihood and raise their quality of life near their places of residence and hence act as a \ndeterrent to distress migration of rural population to cities.  The details are as under: \n  \n\ni. Under Mahatma Gandhi National Rural Employment Guarantee Scheme \n(MGNREGS), guaranteed wage employment of 100 days in a financial year is \nprovided to a rural household whose adult members are willing to take up manual \nwork. Further, an additional 50 days of wage employment is provided over and above \n100 days in the notified drought affected areas or natural calamity affected areas in \nthe country on recommendation of the Ministry of Agriculture and Farmers Welfare. \nii. Deendayal Antyodaya Yojana �� National Rural Livelihoods Mission (DAY -NRLM) \nis implemented with the objective of providing self -employment to the women \nmembers of the rural poor families by mobilizing them into Self Help Groups(SHGs).  \niii. Rural Self Employment and Training Institutes (RSETIs ) provides for skill \ndevelopment programme for self employment. This training with access to Bank loans \nhelps in creating self-employment opportunities at local level.  \niv. Shyama Prasad Mukherji Rurban Mission (SPMRM) is aimed at developing 300   \nrural growth ����������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������\nin all  States and UTs with the objective to bridge the rural urban divide and reduce \nmigration from rural to urban areas and eventually facilitate reverse migration. \nv. Pradhan Mantri Gram Sadak Yojana  (PMGSY) is aimed at providing connectivity by \nway of an all -weather road to eligible unconnected habitations in rural areas so that \nthese habitations can have access to basic health services, education and markets for \ntheir produce. \n****** \n ",
    "ls|LS17_S7_U_292": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF LAW AND JUSTICE \nLEGISLATIVE DEPARTMENT \n \nLOK SABHA \n \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. 292 \n \nTO BE ANSWERED ON FRIDAY, 17th  DECEMBER 2021 \n \nMISUSE OF VOTING MACHINES \n \n \n*292. SHRI SADASHIV KISAN LOKHANDE : \n \nWill the Minister of LAW AND JUSTICE be pleased to state:  \n \n(a) the number of cases regarding misuse of voting machines came \nto notice during the last five years and till date, State-wise; \n(b) the action taken by the Government in this regard; \n(c) whether the Government proposes to adopt the methodolo gy \nbeing used by the developed countries for voting in place of \nvoting machines; \n(d) if so, the details thereof; and \n(e) if not, the reasons therefor? \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF LAW AND JUSTICE  \n(SHRI KIREN RIJIJU) \n \n(a) to (e): A statement is laid on the Table of the House.  \n \n\n \nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN RESPECT OF PART (a) TO (e) OF THE \nLOK SABHA STARRED QUESTION NO. *292 \nDATED 17th DECEMBER, 2021 \n \n(a): The Election Commission of India (ECI) has informed that no such cases \nhave been reported. \n(b): Does not arise in view of (a) above. \n(c): No Sir, no such proposal is under consideration. \n(d): Does not arise in view of (c) above. \n(e) The ECI has informed that voting machines used by it are designed to \nmeet the requirements of electoral laws/process, being followed in India. \nAccording to ECI, EVMs are fully secure due to its technical and \nadministrative safeguards. \n \n******* ",
    "ls|LS17_S7_U_293": " \n \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF HEALTH AND FAMILY WELFARE \nDEPARTMENT OF HEALTH AND FAMILY WELFARE \n \nLOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO.293 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON THE 17TH DECEMBER, 2021 \n \n \nEXPORT OF COVID VACCINE \n \n*293. SHRIMATI MANEKA SANJAY GANDHI:  \n \nWill the Minister of HEALTH AND FAMILY WELFARE be pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether the increase in the export of COVID vaccines to Omicron affected countries \nis proposed to be met from the unused stock of vaccines with private hospitals and if so, \nthe details thereof;  \n \n(b) whether the Government is planning to ramp up the pr oduction of vaccines in the \ncountry to meet the export demand and if so, the details thereof;  \n \n(c) whether the increase in exports is likely to impact the domestic supply in the \ncountry; and  \n \n(d) if so, the details thereof? \n \n \n \n \n \nANSWER \nTHE MINISTER OF HEALTH AND FAMILY WELFARE  \n(DR. MANSUKH MANDAVIYA) \n \n(a) to (d) A Statement is laid on the Table of the House. \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n\n \n \n \nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO LOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. 293* FOR 17TH DECEMBER, 2021 \n \n(a) to (d) Export of COVID -19 vaccines to other countries, including for Omicron \naffected countries, takes into account and prioritizes domestic production and \nrequirements of National COVID -19 vaccination program.  Indian companies have \nsignificantly enhanced their vaccine production capacity and are in a position to meet \ndomestic requirements of COVID -19 vaccines as also contribute their surplus \nproduction to global Covid-19 vaccination requirements. \n******* \n \n ",
    "ls|LS17_S7_U_294": " \n \n \n \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF HOME AFFAIRS \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 294 \n \nTO BE ANSWERED ON THE 30TH NOVEMBER, 2021/ AGRAHAYANA 9, 1943 (SAKA) \n \nSTATUS OF NRC AND CAA \n \n294.   SHRIMATI MALA ROY: \n \nWill the Minister of HOME AFFAIRS be pleased to state:  \n \n(a) the status of having a National Register of  Citizens (NRC) for the entire \ncountry; \n \n(b) the time by when the work related to NRC will  be done along with the status \nof NRC in Assam; and \n \n(c) the time by which the Citizenship Amendment Act (CAA) will be finalised? \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF HOME AFFAIRS \n \n(SHRI NITYANAND RAI) \n \n(a) & (b) : Till now, the Government has not taken any decision to prepare \nNational Register of Indian Citizens (NRIC) at the National level. As far as Assam \nis concerned, on the direction of the Hon’ble Supreme Court, the hard copies of \nthe supplementary list of inclusions and online family -wise list of exclusions in \nNRC have been published on 31st August, 2019.   \n(c): The Citizenship Amendment Act (CAA)  has already been notified on \n12.12.2019. \n***** \n \n ",
    "ls|LS17_S7_U_295": " \n \n \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF HEALTH AND FAMILY WELFARE \nDEPARTMENT OF HEALTH AND FAMILY WELFARE \n \nLOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO.295 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON THE 17TH DECEMBER, 2021 \n \n \n \nCOVID-19 CASES AMONG HEALTHCARE WORKERS \n \n*295. SHRI BRIJENDRA SINGH:  \n \nWill the Minister of HEALTH AND FAMILY WELFARE be pleased to state:  \n \n(a) the estimated number of healthcare workers, including doctors, nurses and lab \ntechnicians in the country, State/UT-wise; and  \n \n(b) the number and details of healthcare workers who contracted COVID-19 between \nMarch, 2021 and June, 2021? \n \n \nANSWER \nTHE MINISTER OF HEALTH AND FAMILY WELFARE  \n(DR. MANSUKH MANDAVIYA) \n \n(a) & (b) A Statement is laid on the Table of the House. \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n\n \n \n \n \nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO LOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. 295* FOR 17TH DECEMBER, 2021 \n \n(a) & (b) As on 14th December 2021, a total of 1,04,09,651 Health Care Workers \nincluding doctors, nurses and lab technicians are registered on Co -WIN portal. The \nState/UT-wise details are at Annexure-1. \n    Data on Healthcare Workers who contracted COVID -19 is expected to be \nmaintained by respective States/UTs and are not maintained Centrally. Government \nof India has given first priority to Health Care Workers for COVID -19 vaccination \nso that they are conferr ed full protection provided by vaccine against COVID -19. \nThe Covid vaccination in India started on 16th January 2021 and the first group taken \nup for this vaccination is the Healthcare Workers. \n \n***** \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n\n \nAnnexure-1 \nState/UT-wise number of Health Care Workers registered on Co-WIN Portal \n(As on 14th December 2021) \nS. No. State/UT No. of Registered Health Care Workers \n1 Andaman and Nicobar Islands 5,505 \n2 Andhra Pradesh 4,89,693 \n3 Arunachal Pradesh 23,760 \n4 Assam 2,18,481 \n5 Bihar 5,29,808 \n6 Chandigarh 26,526 \n7 Chhattisgarh 3,15,494 \n8 Dadra and Nagar Haveli 5,008 \n9 Daman and Diu 2,209 \n10 Delhi 2,66,195 \n11 Goa 25,507 \n12 Gujarat 6,30,917 \n13 Haryana 2,56,040 \n14 Himachal Pradesh 94,399 \n15 Jammu and Kashmir 1,06,275 \n16 Jharkhand 2,10,581 \n17 Karnataka 7,86,705 \n18 Kerala 5,50,386 \n19 Ladakh 5,627 \n20 Lakshadweep 1,297 \n21 Madhya Pradesh 4,98,899 \n22 Maharashtra 13,17,594 \n23 Manipur 41,172 \n24 Meghalaya 32,328 \n25 Mizoram 18,470 \n26 Nagaland 16,580 \n27 Odisha 3,56,620 \n28 Puducherry 38,840 \n29 Punjab 2,23,068 \n30 Rajasthan 5,62,650 \n31 Sikkim 11,905 \n32 Tamil Nadu 6,05,455 \n33 Telangana 3,17,314 \n34 Tripura 54,106 \n35 Uttar Pradesh 9,83,733 \n36 Uttarakhand 1,21,362 \n37 West Bengal 6,59,142 \n38 Total 1,04,09,651 \n ",
    "ls|LS17_S7_U_296": " \n \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF WOMEN & CHILD DEVELOPMENT \n  \nLOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO: 296 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 17.12.2021 \n  \nREVIEW OF ANGANWADI CENTRES \n  \n*296:  MS. DIYA KUMARI \n  \nWill the Minister of WOMEN AND CHILD DEVELOPMENT be pleased to state:  \n  \n(a) whether the Government conducts periodic review of the condition of Anganwadi \nCentres such as provision of adequate storage facilities; \n(b) if so, the details thereof and the steps taken by the Government to address the \nissues identified particularly in the light of POSHAN Abhiyan; \n(c) whether funds have been allocated for infrastructural development and \nmaintenance of Anganwadi Centres under POSHAN 2.0 Abhiyan and if so, the \ndetails thereof; and \n(d) the steps taken by the Government to ensure efficient functioning of these \nCentres in the country especially in Rajasthan? \n  \n  \nANSWER \n  \nMINISTER OF WOMEN AND CHILD DEVELOPMENT \n(SHRIMATI SMRITI ZUBIN IRANI) \n  \n  \n(a) to (d): A Statement is laid on the Table of the House. \n   \n***** \n  \n  \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n\n \nStatement referred to in reply to Part (a) to (d) of Lok Sabha Starred Question \nNo.296 by Ms.Diya Kumari to be answered on 17.12.2021 regarding “Review of \nAnganwadi Centres” \n  \n(a) to (d) The Government has ensured sustained engagements with State \nGovernments/UT Administrations for review and improvement of Anganwadi Centres \nincluding recent interactions to discuss different implementation related issues to \nstrengthen services under Anganwa di Services Scheme. Further, streamlined \nguidelines have also been issued on 13.01.2021, covering several aspects such as \nquality assurance, roles and responsibilities of duty holders, procedure for procurement, \nintegrating AYUSH concepts and Data manageme nt and monitoring through “Poshan \nTracker” for transparency, efficiency and accountability in delivery of Supplementary \nNutrition under the Scheme. \n  \nFurther, the Government of India, time and again, reviews the implementation of \nAnganwadi Services Scheme and the facilities provided therein, with States/UTs \nthrough various modes i.e. letters/reminders/conferences/video conferences and field \nvisits by officials of Central Government. \n  \nAn amount of Rs.9315.26 lakhs (GoI Share) has been allocated to States/UT s under \nthe Annual Programme Implementation Plan (APIP) 2021 -22 for maintenance of \nAnganwadi Centres under Mission POSHAN 2.0. \n  \n***** \n ",
    "ls|LS17_S7_U_297": " \n \n \nGOVERNEMNT OF INDIA\n \nMINISTRY OF AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE\n \nDEPARTMENT OF AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE\n \n \nLOK SABHA\n \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.\n297\n \n \nTO BE ANSWERED ON THE 30\nth\n \nNOVEMBER,\n \n2021\n \n \nEXPANSION OF SCOPE OF PM\n-\nKISAN SCHEME\n \n \n297\n.\n \nSHRI PINAKI MISRA\n:\n \n \n \nWill the Minister of Agriculture and Farmers Welfare\n \nकृ \u0000ष\n \nएवं\n \n\u0000कसान\n \nक\u0000याण\n \nमं\u0000ी\n \nbe pleased \nto state:\n \n(a)\n \nwhether the Ministry has plans to include landless agricultural labourers\n, \nsharecroppers and vulnerable agricultural households under the PM\n-\nKISAN Schemes, \n \n \n(b)\n \nif so, the details of the steps taken and if not, the reason\ns\n \ntherefor; and \n \n \n(c)\n \nwhether the Government is considering to increase the quantum of assistance \nunder the\n \nsaid Scheme, if so, the details about the increment including the expected date \nof its implementation and if not, the reasons therefor? \n \n \nANSWER\n \n \nMINISTER OF AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE \n \nकृ \u0000ष\n \nएवं\n \n\u0000कसान\n \nक\u0000याण\n \nमं\u0000ी\n \n        \n(SHRI NARENDRA SINGH TOMAR)\n \n \n(a) & (b):\n \nLandholding\n, irrespective of the size of the land,\n \nis the basic eligibility \ncriterion for receiving benefits under the Scheme. \nUnder the PM\n-\nKISAN Scheme, financial \nbenefits of Rs. 6000/\n-\n \nannually is being given in three equal insta\nllments of Rs. 2000/\n-\n \neach, every\n \nfour months to all the \nlandholding farmer families\n, subject\n \nto certain exclusion \ncriteria, through\n \ndirect benefit transfer mode once their correct and verified data is received \nfrom respective \nState/UT Governments on PM\n-\nK\nI\nSAN \nportal and its subsequent validation \nthrough Aadhar/PFMS/Income Tax database. \n \nAt present, there is no proposal\n \nto include landless agricultural labourers and \nsharecroppers under the ambit of Scheme \n \n(c):\n \nAs of now\n, there is no\n \nproposal to i\nncrease the\n \nquantum of assistance\n \ngiven under \nPM\n-\nKISAN Scheme\n.\n \n \n \n****** \n ",
    "ls|LS17_S7_U_298": "             \n  \n   \n \n \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA\n \nMINISTRY OF AGRICULTURE & FARMERS WELFARE\n \nDEPARTMENT OF AGRICULTURE & FARMERS WELFARE\n \n \nLOK\n \nSABHA\n \nUN\nSTARRED QUESTION NO. \n298\n \nTO BE ANSWERED ON THE \n30\nTH\n \nNOVEMBER\n, 2021\n \n \nSURVEY OF FARMERS INCOMES\n \n \n298\n.\n \n  \nSHRI\n \nSAPTAGIRI\n \nSANKAR ULAKA\n:\n \nWill the Minister of \nAGRICULTURE & FARMERS WELFARE\n \n \nकृ \u0000ष\n \nएवं\n \n\u0000कसान\n \nक\u0000याण\n \nमं\u0000ी\n \n \nbe pleased to state:\n \n \n(\na\n)\n \nwhether the Government has put into place any\n \nannual surveys to measure \nfarmers’ expenditure and\n \nfarmers’ incomes in the country;\n \n \n(\nb\n)\n \nif so,\n \ndetails of the same including information\n \non the current monthly farmer \nincomes on average in\n \neach State vis\n-\nà\n-\nvis their monthly expenditure; and\n \n \n(c) \n \nif not, the details of the methodology devised to\n \nmonitor progress of the \nGovernment‘s commitment to\n \ndo\nuble farmers’ incomes?\n \n \n \nANSWER\n \n \nMINIST\nER\n \nOF AGRICULTURE \nAND\n \nFARMERS WELFARE\n  \n \nकृ \u0000ष\n \nएवं\n \n\u0000कसान\n \nक\u0000याण\n \nमं\u0000ी\n \n   \n \n(SHRI \nNARENDRA SINGH TOMAR\n)\n \n \n(a) and (b): National Statistical Office (NSO) \n \nin its 77\nth\n \nround \ncarried out a survey on \nSituation Assessment of \nAgricultural Households\n \nand \nLand and Livestock Holdings of \nHouseholds in the rural areas of the country with an integrated schedule of enquiry during the \nperiod January 2019 to December 2019.\n \nAs per \nthe survey results\n, the \nState / UT wise \naverage \n \nmonthly \nincome and \naverage expenses on different heads\n \nper agricultural \nhousehold\n \nis given i\nn \nAnnexure\n-\nI & Annexure\n-\nII\n \nre\ns\npectively.\n \n \n(c): \n \nThe Government constituted an Inter\n-\nministerial Committee in April, 2016 to \nexamine issues relating to “Doubling of Farmers \nIncome” and recommend strategies to \nachieve the same. The Committee submitted its Report to the Government in September, \n2018 containing the strategy for doubling of farmers’ income by the year 2022. After \nacceptance of the Doubling of Farmers Income (DFI)\n \nCommittee recommendations, the \nGovernment has constituted an ‘Empowered Body’ to review and monitor the progress.\n \n \n \n \n \n \n\nAnnexure\n \nI\n \n \nAnnexure referred in reply to part (a) and (b) of Lok Sabha Unstarred Q. No. 298 due \nfor answer on 30/11/2021\n \n \nState /UTs \nwise\n \nAverage monthly income per agricultural household during agricultural year \nJuly 2018 \n-\n \nJune 2019\n \n \nState/ Group of UTs\n \nAverage monthly income (\n₹)\n \n \nAndhra Pradesh\n \n10,480\n \nArunachal Pradesh\n \n19,225\n \nAssam\n \n10,675\n \nBihar\n \n7,542\n \nChhattisgarh\n \n9,677\n \nGujarat\n \n12,631\n \nHaryana\n \n22,841\n \nHimachal Pradesh\n \n12,153\n \nJammu & Kashmir\n \n18,918\n \nJharkhand\n \n4,895\n \nKarnataka\n \n13,441\n \nKerala\n \n17,915\n \nMadhya Pradesh\n \n8,339\n \nMaharashtra\n \n11,492\n \nManipur\n \n11,227\n \nMeghalaya\n \n29,348\n \nMizoram\n \n17,964\n \nNagaland\n \n9,877\n \nOdisha\n \n5,112\n \nPunjab\n \n26,701\n \nRajasthan\n \n12,520\n \nSikkim\n \n12,447\n \nTamil Nadu\n \n11,924\n \nTelangana\n \n9,403\n \nTripura\n \n9,918\n \nUttarakhand\n \n13,552\n \nUttar Pradesh\n \n8,061\n \nWest Bengal\n \n6,762\n \nGroup of N E States \n \n16,863\n \nGroup of UTs\n \n18,511\n \nall India\n \n10,218\n \n \nSource: NSS Report No. 587: \nSituation Assessment of Agricultural Households and Land \nand Livestock Holdings of Households in Rural India, 2019\n \n \n \n \n \n\nAnnexure\n \nII\n \nAnnexure referred in reply to part (a) and (b) of Lok Sabha Unstarred Q. No. 298 due \nfor answer on 30/11/2021\n \n \nState /UTs \nwise\n \nAverage monthly \nexpenses\n \nper agricultural household \non different heads\n \nduring agricultural year July 2018 \n-\n \nJune 2019\n \n \n \nState/ Group of UTs\n \nAverage monthly \npaid out \nexpenditure for \ncrop production(\n₹)\n \nAverage monthly \npaid out expenditure \nfor farming of\n \nanimals (\n₹)\n \n \nAndhra Pradesh\n \n8,847\n \n3,072\n \n \nArunachal Pradesh\n \n1,090\n \n1,173\n \n \nAssam\n \n1,202\n \n348\n \n \nBihar\n \n2,305\n \n859\n \n \nChhattisgarh\n \n1,996\n \n84\n \n \nGujarat\n \n2,417\n \n2,194\n \n \nHaryana\n \n11,190\n \n4,451\n \n \nHimachal Pradesh\n \n1,244\n \n783\n \n \nJammu & Kashmir\n \n998\n \n604\n \nJharkhand\n \n753\n \n156\n \n \nKarnataka\n \n3,219\n \n1,229\n \n \nKerala\n \n2,508\n \n3,191\n \n \nMadhya Pradesh\n \n3,038\n \n769\n \n \nMaharashtra\n \n3,500\n \n1,490\n \n \nManipur\n \n1,221\n \n1,596\n \n \nMeghalaya\n \n2,293\n \n381\n \n \nMizoram\n \n320\n \n2,037\n \n \nNagaland\n \n172\n \n289\n \nOdisha\n \n1,587\n \n392\n \n \nPunjab\n \n11,277\n \n3,118\n \n \nRajasthan\n \n2,361\n \n1,773\n \n \nSikkim\n \n363\n \n523\n \n \nTamil Nadu\n \n2,826\n \n1,880\n \nTelangana\n \n6,543\n \n1,163\n \n \nTripura\n \n1,441\n \n527\n \nUttarakhand\n \n2,319\n \n887\n \n \nUttar Pradesh\n \n2,367\n \n1,066\n \n \nWest Bengal\n \n2,457\n \n454\n \nGroup of UTs\n \n1243\n \n1993\n \n \nAll India\n \n2,959\n \n1,267\n \nSource: NSS Report No. 587: Situation Assessment of \nAgricultural Households and Land \nand Livestock Holdings of Households in Rural India, 2019\n \n \n**********\n ",
    "ls|LS17_S7_U_299": " \n \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA\n \nMINISTRY OF AGRICULTURE & FARMERS WELFARE\n \nDEPARTMENT OF AGRICULTURE & FARMERS WELFARE\n \n \n \n \nLOK SABHA\n \n \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 299\n \nTO BE ANSWERED ON THE \n3\n0\nth\n \nNOVEMBER\n, 2021\n \n \n                \nMSP FOR RABI CROPS\n              \n \n \n299\n. \n \n      \nSHRI VIJAYAKUMAR (ALIAS) VIJAY VASANTH\n:\n \n               \nSHRI PARTHIBAN S.R.\n:\n        \n      \n \n \nWill the Minister of AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE \nकृ\u0000ष एवं \u0000कसान क\u0000याण मं\u0000ी \nbe pleased to state\n:\n \n(a)\n \n   \nwhether\n \nthe Government has increased Minimum Support Price (MSP) for designated\n \n \n       \nrabi crops for the year 2021 or during the marketing/procurement season 2022\n-\n2023;\n \n \n(b) \n   \nif so, the crop\n-\nwise details of the increase in the MSP thereof;\n \n(c)\n   \n \nwhether the \nGovernment has conducted any study for assessing the impact of MSP and\n  \n \n       \n \nthe benefits likely to be availed by the farmers; and\n \n(d) \n   \nif so, the details thereof and if not, the reasons therefor ?\n \n \nANSWER\n \n \nTHE MINISTER\n \nOF AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE\n \n \nकृ\n\u0000ष एवं \u0000कसान क\u0000याण मं\u0000ी    \n \n(\nSHRI NARENDRA SINGH TOMAR)\n \n \n(a)\n:     \n \nGovernment has recently announced Minimum Support Prices (MSP) for 6 mandated \nrabi crops viz. wheat, barley, gram, masur (lentil), rapeseed &\n \nmustard and safflower, for \nRabi Marketing Season (RMS) 2022\n-\n23, on the basis of the recommendations of the \nCommission for Agricultural Costs and Prices (CACP), and after considering the views of \nState Governments and Central Ministries/Departments concern\ned and other relevant \nfactors.\n \n \n(b):      \nMSP for mandated rabi crops for RMS 2022\n-\n23 is given at \nAnnex\n.\n \n \n(c) & (d)\n:     \n \nThe NITI Aayog (erstwhile Planning Commission) has conducted a study entitled \n“Efficacy of minimum support prices on farmers”, 2016. T\nhe study covered 14 States, 36 \nDistricts, 72 Blocks, 144 Villages and 1440 Households. The study is based on both the \nprimary and secondary data. The study has found among other \nthings that\n \nMSP declared by \nthe Government has encouraged 78 % of the farmers \ncovered under the study for adopting \nimproved methods of farming such as high yielding varieties of seeds, organic manure, \nchemical fertilizer, pesticides and improved methods of harvesting etc. \n \n \n\n \n \nAnnex\n \n \nAnnex ref\nerred to in reply to part (b)\n \nof \nLok\n \nSabha Unstarred Question No.\n \n299 \ndue for \nreply on \n30.11\n.2021\n \n \n \nMinimum Support Price \n(MSP) \nfor Rabi Marketing Season 2022\n-\n23\n \n                                                                                                      \n \n                                                                                                                             \n  \n                                                                                                 \nSl. No.\n \nCOMMODITY \n \nMSP\n \n(\n₹\n/\nquintal\n)\n \n1\n \nWHEAT\n \n2015\n \n2\n \nBARLEY\n \n1635\n \n3\n \nGRAM\n \n5230\n \n4\n \nMASUR (LENTIL)\n \n5500\n \n5\n \nRAPESEED & MUSTARD\n \n5050\n \n6\n \nSAFFLOWER\n \n5441\n \n ",
    "ls|LS17_S7_U_300": "O.I.H\n \nGOVERNEMNT OF INDIA\n \nMINISTRY OF AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE\n \nDEPART\nMENT OF AGRICULTURE\n \nAND FARMERS WELFARE\n \n \nLOK SABHA\n \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.300 \n \nTO BE ANSWERED ON THE 30\nth\n \nNOVEMBER ,2021\n \n \nPM\n-\nKISAN Scheme Transactions\n \n \n300\n.\n \nSHRI MITESH RAMESHBHAI \nPATEL:\n \nSHRI SRINIWAS PATIL:\n \nSHRI BALUBHAU ALIAS SURESH NARAYAN DHANORKAR:\n \nSHRI K. NAVASKANI: \n \n \n \nWill the Minister of Agriculture and Farmers Welfare\n \nकृ \u0000ष\n \nएवं\n \n\u0000कसान\n \nक\u0000याण\n \nमं\u0000ी\n \nbe \npleased to state:\n \n(a)\n \nthe total number of Pradhan Mantri Kisan SammanNidhi\n \n(PM\n-\nKISAN) Yojana \nbeneficiaries and transactions that have taken place since its launch till November, \n2021, month\n-\nwise and State\n-\nwise;\n \n \n(b)\n \nthe total number of PM\n-\nKISAN scheme transactions which have failed since its \nlaunch till November, 2021, month\n-\nwis\ne and State\n-\nwise;\n \n \n(c)\n \nwhether the Government has identified the reasons for failed transactions;\n \n \n(d)\n \nif so, the details thereof and if not, the reasons therefor;\n \n \n(e)\n \nthe\n \nsteps being taken to ensure that beneficiaries receive their installments in \nsituations where transactions have failed; and\n \n \n(f)\n \nif not, the reasons therefor?\n \n \nANSWER\n \n \nMINISTER OF AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE \n \nकृ \u0000ष\n \nएवं\n \n\u0000कसान\n \nक\u0000याण\n \nमं\u0000ी\n(SHRI NARENDRA\n \nSINGH TOMAR)\n \n \n(a) & (b):\n \nAs of 25.11.2021, more than 11.60 crore beneficiaries have been given \nthe benefits of PM\n-\nKISAN Scheme. Under PM\n-\nKISAN Scheme the benefits are not \ntransferrable every month. The benefits are transferred in three equal installments \nof \nRs.2000/\n-\n \neach in every four\n-\nmonths.  The total number of transactions done under PM\n-\nKISAN Scheme has been 80,27,44,674 of which the number of failed transaction is less \nthan 1%. The cumulative number of PM\n-\nKISAN Scheme transactions which have failed \nsi\nnce 1\nst\n \nFebruary, 2019 till 31\nst\n \nOctober, 2021 is \n69,000,58 o\nut of which 31,86,921 \ntransactions were successfully reprocessed.\n \nThe State\n-\nwise details of number of total \ntransactions, total failed transactions and total successfully re\n-\nprocesse\nd transaction\ns \nunder PM\n-\nKISAN Scheme\nhave been placed\n \nat \nAnnexure\n-\nI.\n \n \n\n \n(c)& (d):\n \nVarious reasons have been identified by the Union Government for the \ntransactions failure under PM\n-\nKISAN Scheme such as Account closed/Transferred, \nInvalid IFSC\n,\n \nAccount Inactive, Account Dormant, Amount exceeds limit set on Account \nby Bank for Credit/Debit per transaction, Account Holder Expired, Account Blocked or \nFrozen, Inactive Aadhar, Network failure\n, technical glitches\n \netc.  \n \n(e):\n \nTo tackle the issues of tr\nansaction failures and \nre\n-\nprocess the payment to such \nregistered farmers’ families, a Standard Operating Procedure (SOP) has been \ndeveloped and issued to \nall \nStates/UTs for necessary action at their end.  In cases \nwhere the corrective measures are to be ta\nken by States/UTs, such transaction failure \nrecords are opened to respective State/UT for correction under the “Correction Module” \ntab of PM\n-\nKISAN portal. Thereafter, all the transaction failure records are reprocessed \nfor the payment of the respective ins\ntallment under PM\n-\nKISAN Scheme. The \nDepartment through weekly video conferencing take up the matter with State/UT \nGovernments to expedite the resolution of the issue and reprocess the failed \ntransactions for the payments of the respective installments unde\nr PM\n-\nKISAN Scheme. \n \n(f):\n \nDoes \nnot \narise in view of above. \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n\n \nAnnexure\n-\nI\n \nThe state\n-\nwise details of total transactions, failed transactions and reprocessed transactions \nsince inception of the PM\n-\nKISAN Scheme till 31\nst\n \nOctober, 2021\n \nState\n \nTotal number of \ntransactions \n \nTotal Transaction \nFailed\n \nTotal Reprocess \nTransaction Success\n \nANDAMAN AND \nNICOBAR ISLANDS\n \n140,646\n \n9,368\n \n8,329\n \nANDHRA PRADESH\n \n40,876,498\n \n323,810\n \n87,224\n \nARUNACHAL PRADESH\n \n618,650\n \n1,978\n \n456\n \nASSAM\n \n15,699,419\n \n59,719\n \n2,908\n \nBIHAR\n \n54,321,284\n \n166,193\n \n72,827\n \nCHANDIGARH\n \n3,077\n \n10\n \n-\n \nCHHATTISGARH\n \n20,198,857\n \n84,459\n \n9,884\n \nDELHI\n \n108,076\n \n511\n \n80\n \nGOA\n \n70,541\n \n813\n \n71\n \nGUJARAT\n \n45,535,360\n \n243,171\n \n47,851\n \nHARYANA\n \n15,055,994\n \n93,497\n \n25,994\n \nHIMACHAL PRADESH\n \n7,671,651\n \n48,670\n \n6,382\n \nJAMMU AND \nKASHMIR\n \n8,602,606\n \n45,501\n \n4,641\n \nJHARKHAND\n \n13,554,283\n \n60,869\n \n28,404\n \nKARNATAKA\n \n40,541,798\n \n354,120\n \n70,617\n \nKERALA\n \n28,344,460\n \n119,083\n \n40,126\n \nLADAKH\n \n116,521\n \n1,763\n \n5\n \nLAKSHADWEEP\n \n6,211\n \n10\n \n-\n \nMADHYA PRADESH\n \n58,637,915\n \n1,754,015\n \n1,278,319\n \nMAHARASHTRA\n \n81,065,112\n \n1,198,543\n \n752,295\n \nMANIPUR\n \n2,204,730\n \n21,339\n \n1,042\n \nMEGHALAYA\n \n1,148,367\n \n3,265\n \n822\n \nMIZORAM\n \n857,902\n \n2,184\n \n410\n \nNAGALAND\n \n1,496,519\n \n7,882\n \n3,774\n \nODISHA\n \n26,116,498\n \n309,923\n \n106,909\n \nPUDUCHERRY\n \n87,229\n \n258\n \n109\n \nPUNJAB\n \n16,908,682\n \n131,053\n \n7,758\n \nRAJASTHAN\n \n53,235,200\n \n277,098\n \n96,749\n \nSIKKIM\n \n54,101\n \n225\n \n1\n \nTAMIL NADU\n \n33,307,107\n \n151,016\n \n53,757\n \nTELANGANA\n \n30,866,151\n \n122,365\n \n8,339\n \nTHE DADRA AND \nNAGAR HAVELI AND \nDAMAN AND DIU\n \n96,851\n \n404\n \n36\n \nTRIPURA\n \n1,805,809\n \n4,653\n \n134\n \nUTTAR PRADESH\n \n188,848,925\n \n1,237,309\n \n451,456\n \nUTTARAKHAND\n \n6,958,159\n \n53,708\n \n19,212\n \nWEST BENGAL\n \n7,583,485\n \n11,273\n \n-\n \n\nGrand Total\n \n802,744,674\n \n6,900,058\n \n3,186,921\n \n******\n ",
