    "ls|LS17_S7_U_423": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF HOME AFFAIRS \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 423 \n \nTO BE ANSWERED ON THE 30th NOVEMBER, 2021/AGRAHAYANA 9,1943 (SAKA) \n \nCYBER CRIMES \n \n423.  MS. RAMYA HARIDAS: \nDR. NISHIKANT DUBEY: \n \nWill the Minister of HOME AFFAIRS be pleased to state:  \n \n(a) whether the Government is aware that the cyber  crimes in the country have \nincreased in the past few years; \n \n(b) if so, the number of cases reported during the last five years, State-wise; \n \n(c) whether the Government has assessed the effects  of increasing cyber \ncrimes on the economic development of the country; \n \n(d) whether the Union Government has taken any  concrete steps to check such \ncyber crimes in the country; and \n \n(e) if so, the details thereof? \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF HOME AFFAIRS \n(SHRI AJAY KUMAR MISHRA) \n(a) to (e): With the enhanced use of cyber space, the number of cyber crimes is \nalso increasing. The National Crime Records Bureau (NCRB) compiles and \npublishes statistical data on crimes in its publication “Crime in India”.  The \nlatest published report is  for the year 2020.   As per the data published by \nNational Crime Records Bureau (NCRB), the details of cases, registered under \ncyber crimes, States/UTs wise, during 2016-2020 are at Annexure.  \n\n--2-- \nLS.US.Q.NO. 423 FOR 30.11.2021 \n „Police‟ and „Public Order‟ are State subjects as per the Seventh Schedule \nof the Constitution of India. States/UTs are primarily responsible for the \nprevention, detection, investigation and prosecution of crimes including cyber \ncrime through their Law Enforcement Agencies (LEAs) .  The LEAs take legal \naction as per provisions of law against the offenders. To strengthen the \nmechanism to deal with cyber crimes, the Central Government has taken steps \nwhich inter-alia include, spreading awareness about cyber crimes; issuance of \nalerts/advisories; capacity building/training of law enforcement personnel/ \npublic prosecutors/ judicial officers; improving cyber forensic facilities, setting \nup of Indian Cyber Crime Coordination Centre (I4C) to provide a framework and \neco-system for LEAs to d eal with the cyber crimes in a comprehensive and \ncoordinated manner; etc.  The Government has launched the National Cyber \nCrime Reporting Portal (www.cybercrime.gov.in), to enable public to report \nincidents pertaining to all types of cyber crimes, with a s pecial focus on cyber \ncrimes against women and children. Citizen Financial Cyber Fraud Reporting \nand Management System has been launched for immediate reporting of \nfinancial frauds and to stop siphoning off funds by the fraudsters.  A toll -free \nnumber 1552 60 has been operationalised to get assistance in lodging online \ncyber complaints.   \n***** \n\n LS.US.Q.NO. 423 FOR 30.11.2021 \n        Annexure \nState/UT-wise Cases Registered (CR) under Cyber Crimes during 2016-2020 \n \n \n \n \nSl.No. State/UT 2016 2017 2018 2019 2020 \nCR CR CR CR CR \n1 Andhra Pradesh 616 931 1207 1886 1899 \n2 Arunachal Pradesh 4 1 7 8 30 \n3 Assam 696 1120 2022 2231 3530 \n4 Bihar 309 433 374 1050 1512 \n5 Chhattisgarh 90 171 139 175 297 \n6 Goa 31 13 29 15 40 \n7 Gujarat 362 458 702 784 1283 \n8 Haryana 401 504 418 564 656 \n9 Himachal Pradesh 31 56 69 76 98 \n10 Jharkhand 259 720 930 1095 1204 \n11 Karnataka 1101 3174 5839 12020 10741 \n12 Kerala 283 320 340 307 426 \n13 Madhya Pradesh 258 490 740 602 699 \n14 Maharashtra 2380 3604 3511 4967 5496 \n15 Manipur 11 74 29 4 79 \n16 Meghalaya 39 39 74 89 142 \n17 Mizoram 1 10 6 8 13 \n18 Nagaland 2 0 2 2 8 \n19 Odisha 317 824 843 1485 1931 \n20 Punjab 102 176 239 243 378 \n21 Rajasthan 941 1304 1104 1762 1354 \n22 Sikkim 1 1 1 2 0 \n23 Tamil Nadu 144 228 295 385 782 \n24 Telangana 593 1209 1205 2691 5024 \n25 Tripura 8 7 20 20 34 \n26 Uttar Pradesh 2639 4971 6280 11416 11097 \n27 Uttarakhand 62 124 171 100 243 \n28 West Bengal 478 568 335 524 712 \n  TOTAL STATE(S) 12159 21530 26931 44511 49708 \n29 A&N Islands 3 3 7 2 5 \n30 Chandigarh 26 32 30 23 17 \n31 D&N Haveli and Daman & Diu+ 1 1 0 3 3 \n32 Delhi 98 162 189 115 168 \n33 Jammu & Kashmir* 28 63 73 73 120 \n34 Ladakh - - - - 1 \n35 Lakshadweep 0 0 4 4 3 \n36 Puducherry 2 5 14 4 10 \n  TOTAL UT(S) 158 266 317 224 327 \n  TOTAL (ALL INDIA) 12317 21796 27248 44735 50035 \nSource: Crime in India      \nNote : '+' Combined data of erstwhile D&N Haveli UT and Daman & Diu UT for 2016 -2019 \n*' Data of erstwhile Jammu & Kashmir State including Ladakh for 2016-2019 ",
    "ls|LS17_S7_U_424": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF HOME AFFAIRS \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 424 \n \nTO BE ANSWERED ON THE 30th NOVEMBER, 2021/AGRAHAYANA 9,1943 (SAKA) \n \nTERROR ATTACKS IN MANIPUR  \n \n424.  SHRI SANGANNA AMARAPPA: \nSHRI L.S. TEJASVI SURYA: \nSHRI PRATHAP SIMHA: \nDR. UMESH G. JADHAV: \n \nWill the Minister of HOME AFFAIRS be pleased to state:  \n \n(a) whether the Government has taken any  cognisance of the recent terror \nattacks in Manipur by  heavily-armed insurgent groups and if so, the details  \nthereof; \n \n(b) whether the Government intelligence reveals any  kind of involvement of \nforeign hand in the insurgent attacks in North-east of the country; and \n \n(c) if so, the details thereof? \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF HOME AFFAIRS \n(SHRI NITYANAND RAI) \n(a): An extensive operation was conducted in the surrounding areas by Security \nForces to nab the insurgents involved in the recent terror attack on Assam \nRifles convoy on November 13, 2021. Police Station Singhat, District \nChurachandpur (Manipur) had lodged a case  in this regard, Which has been \ntransferred to National Investigation Agency (NIA) for investigation. \n(b)&(c): There is no specific information indicating any kind of involvement of \nforeign hand in the attack. \n****** ",
    "ls|LS17_S7_U_425": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA\n \nMINISTRY OF AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE\n \nDEPARTMENT OF AGRICULTURE \nAND FARMERS WELFARE\n \nLOK SABHA\n \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. \n425\n \nTO BE ANSWERED ON THE \n30\nth \nNOVEMBER\n, 2021\n \n \nPROMOTION OF AGRO\n-\nFORESTRY\n \n425.\n   \nSHRI \nCHANDRA PRAKASH JOSHI\n:\n \n          \nSHRIMATI JASKAUR MEENA:\n \nWill the Minister of \n \nAGRICULTURE \nAND\n \nFARMERS \n  \nWELFARE\n \n \nकृ \u0000ष\n \n \nऔर\n \n\u0000कसान\n \nक\u0000याण\n \nमं\u0000ी\n \nbe pleased to state:\n \n(a)\n \n \nthe details of the schemes being implemented by the Government to promote agro forestry \nin the country\n;\n \n(b) \n \nthe details of the States selected for agro forestry;\n \n(c) \n \nthe details of identified areas for their selection under these schemes including Rajasthan;\n \n(d) \n \nthe time by which this scheme is likely to be initiated along with the details thereof;\n \n(e)\n \nthe det\nails of eligible farmers under such scheme;\n \n(f) \n \nthe details of the subsidies, concessions and facilities provided by the Government on ago \nforestry; and\n \n(g) \n \nthe details of the benefits of the agro forestry?\n \n \nANSWER\n \nMINISTER OF AGRICULTURE \nAND\n \nFARMERS WELFARE\n \nकृ \u0000ष\n \nएवं\n \n\u0000कसान\n \nक\u0000याण\n \nमं\u0000ी\n \n(SHRI NARENDRA SINGH TOMAR)\n \n(a):\n \n   \nSub\n-\nMission on Agroforestry Scheme is being implemented by this Department since \n2016\n-\n17 to encourage tree plantation on farm land \nin a complementary and integrated manner \nwi\nth crops and livestock, \nto help the farmers \nto \nget additional income and make their farming \nsystems more climate resilient and adaptive. \n \n \n(b)&(c):     \nPresently, the scheme is \nbeing \nimplemented in 2\n1\n \nStates viz. Andhra Pradesh, Bihar, \nChhattisgarh, Gujarat, Haryana, Himachal Pradesh, Jharkhand, Ka\nrnataka, Kerala, Madhya \nPradesh\n, Maharashtra, Odisha, Punjab, \nRajasthan\n, Tamil Nadu, Telangana, Uttar Pradesh\n, \nMizoram, Manipur, Meghalaya, Nagalan\nd and 2 UT\ns viz. Jammu & Kashmir\n \nand Ladakh\n.\nThe \nextant scheme is being implemented in those\n \nStates/UTs \nthat have liberalized \nfelling and transit \nregulation\ns\n \nfor selected tree species. Other States/UTs can access the benefit of the scheme as \nand when such relaxation \nis notified by them. \n \n\n(d): \n \nThe extant scheme is already ongoing since 2016\n-\n17. \n \n \n(e): \n \nAll the farmers specially\n \nsmall\n \nmarginal\n \nfarmers are eligible for the benefit of the \nscheme.\n \n \n(f): \n \nAssistance to farmers for various interve\nntions under the scheme is\n \nas \nfollowing\n:\n \n \nSl \nNo\n.\n \nComponent/Activities/Sub \nComponent\n \nCost Norms\n \n( Rs in lakh)\n \nPattern of \nAssistance\n \n1.      Nursery Development\n \n \n100% for \nGovernment \nAgencies and 50% \nfor farmers/private \nagencies.\n \n(a)\n \nSmall Nurseries\n \nRs 10 lakh per \nnursery\n \n(b)\n \nBig \nNurseries\n \nRs 16 lakh per \nnursery\n \n(c) \n \nHi \n–\nTech  Nurseries\n \nRs 40 lakh per \nnursery\n \n2.\n \nPeripheral/Boundary Plantation\n \nRs.70 /plant\n \n100% for \nGovernment \nAgencies and 50% \nfor farmers/private \nagencies in four \nyears in the ratio \nof 40:20:20:20\n \n3.\n \nL\now Density \nPlantation (100\n-\n500 \nH\na)\n \nRs 28,000/H\na\n \n4.       High Density Block Plantation\n \n(a)\n \n500   to 1000 plants/Ha\n \nRs 30,000/Ha\n \n(b)\n \n1000   to 1200 plants/Ha\n \nRs 35,000/Ha\n \n(c)\n \n1200   to 1500 plants/Ha\n \nRs 45,000/Ha\n \n(d)\n \n> 1500 plants/Ha\n \nRs 50,000/Ha\n \n5.\n \nCapacity Building and Training\n \nUpto 5% of total allocation to the States\n \n \n(g)\n:\n \nThe Agro\n-\nforestry\n \nscheme\n \nenvisages a lot of quantifiable benefits \n \nlike additional income, protection from \ncrop damage\n, \n \nhigher\n \ncarbon\n \nsequestration along with climate change \n \nadaptation and mitigation. \n \n \n******\n ",
    "ls|LS17_S7_U_426": "Page 1 of 5 \n \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF YOUTH AFFAIRS & SPORTS \n(DEPARTMENT OF SPORTS) \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 426 \nANSWERED ON 30.11.2021 \n \nSports Infrastructure \n \n426. SHRI RAJIV PRATAP RUDY: \n \nWill the Minister of YOUTH AFFAIRS AND SPORTS be pleased to state: \n \n(a) the details of the existing sports infrastructure in  the country across all \nsports, State-wise, district-wise including Bihar; \n \n(b) the details and the number of coaches available  in the country, State -\nwise, district-wise and sport-wise including Bihar; \n \n(c) whether the Government plans to expand and  improve existing sports \ninfrastructure under Khelo India  and if so, the details thereof, State -wise \nincluding Bihar  with the estimated cost sanctioned and timelines for  \ncompletion of the projects; \n \n(d) whether the Government proposes to expand the  scope of sports \ninfrastructure under Corporate Social  Responsibility (CSR) and if so, the \ndetails thereof; and \n \n(e) the details of other steps taken by the  Government to promote Khelo \nIndia initiatives at the rural and local level across the country particularly in \nthe State of Bihar? \n \nANSWER \nTHE MINISTER OF YOUTH AFFAIRS & SPORTS  \n{ SHRI ANURAG SINGH THAKUR } \n \n(a) and (b) ‘Sports’ being a State subject, the primary responsibility to \ncreate sports infrast ructure and e ngage sports coaches rests with the \nState/Union Territory Governments. The Union Government supplements \ntheir efforts. The sports infrastructure and coaches in various parts of the \ncountry, including Bihar,  are under the jurisdiction of the respective \nbodies/departments of State/UT Governments. As such, the information \nabout them is not maintained in this Ministry. However, this Ministry has \n\nPage 2 of 5 \n \nsanctioned 267 sports infrastructure projects of various categories in the \ncountry under the Khelo India  Scheme, which includes 11 projects in Bihar , \nas detailed in Annexure – I. Further, the Sports Authority of India  (SAI) has \n918 regular and contractual coaches under its various centres across the \ncountry. Discipline-wise and State-wise details of coaches under SAI are at \nAnnexure – II and Annexure – III, respectively. \n \n(c)  Under the “Utilisation and creation/upgradation of sports infrastructure” \nvertical of the Khelo India Scheme, financial assistance is provided to \nState/UT Governments and other eligible entities for creation/upgradation of \nsports infrastructure of  various categories across the countr y. Khelo India \nScheme is a demand driven scheme  under which projects  are sanctioned \nregularly on the basis of proposals received from various State/UT \nGovernments and other eligible entities  from time to time . The timeline of \ncompletion of projects sanctioned under this Scheme is two to three years.  \nHowever, t he responsibility of executing the projects rests with the \nState/UT Governments.  The State -wise detail s of s ports infrastructure \nsanctioned in the country, including Bihar, under the Khelo India Scheme \nare given at Annexure – I. \n \n(d) The Government encourages the contribution of Corporate Social \nResponsibility (CSR) funds for development of sports infrastr ucture through \nvarious Schemes  of this Ministry . Under the National Sports Development \nFund, which is a Central Sector Scheme, CSR funds are received from public \nas well as private institutions for creation and maintenance of sports \ninfrastructure. So far, Rs. 170 crore of CSR funding has been received from \nvarious organisations. \n \n(e) The Government provides financial assistance for development of sports \nby way of  creation/upgradation of sports infrastructure as well as \nconducting competitions and coaching/training camps under the Khelo India \nScheme across the country throughout the year, including the State of \nBihar. So far, an amount of Rs. 55.49 crore has been sanctioned for creation \nof sports infrastructure in various districts of Bihar  under the Scheme . \nFurther, Rs.  1.16 crore ha ve been released under the vertical “sports for \npeace and development” of the Scheme in Bihar. \n  \n \n\nPage 3 of 5 \n \nANNEXURE REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PART S (a) , (b) AND (c)  OF LOK \nSABHA UNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 426 ANSWERED ON 30.11.2021 \nREGARDING “SPORTS INFRASTRUCTURE” ASKED BY SHRI RAJIV PRATAP \nRUDY, HON’BLE MEMBER OF LOK SABHA \n \nANNEXURE – I \n \nState/UT-wise details of projects sanctioned under Khelo India Scheme \n \nS. \nNo. \nState/UT Amount \nSanctioned \n(In ₹ Cr.) \nAmount \nReleased \n(In ₹ Cr.) \nTotal number of \nprojects \n1 Andaman and Nicobar Islands 5.00 0.00 1 \n2 Andhra Pradesh 29.30 15.68 6 \n3 Arunachal Pradesh 157.00 102.90 20 \n4 Assam 43.18 41.68 7 \n5 Bihar 55.49 12.00 11 \n6 Chandigarh 0.00 0.00 0 \n7 Chhattisgarh 21.10 7.03 4 \n8 Dadra & Nagar Haveli and Daman & Diu 0.00 0.00 0 \n9 Delhi 89.84 35.02 8 \n10 Goa 19.10 2.12 4 \n11 Gujarat 608.37 32.13 5 \n12 Haryana 79.67 46.53 8 \n13 Himachal Pradesh 22.10 3.10 5 \n14 Jammu and Kashmir 28.56 11.57 5 \n15 Jharkhand 2.07 2.07 1 \n16 Karnataka 95.31 43.89 14 \n17 Kerala 62.74 28.12 5 \n18 Ladakh 14.28 10.14 3 \n19 Lakshadweep 9.00 0.00 2 \n20 Madhya Pradesh 74.14 34.06 10 \n21 Maharashtra 101.80 53.38 11 \n22 Manipur 80.45 39.03 8 \n23 Meghalaya 28.00 0.00 6 \n24 Mizoram 34.50 22.25 7 \n25 Nagaland 40.00 23.50 6 \n26 Odisha 28.00 22.13 4 \n27 Puducherry 16.02 5.00 3 \n28 Punjab 76.21 41.14 8 \n29 Rajasthan 93.76 72.60 40 \n30 Sikkim 18.83 12.18 4 \n31 Tamil Nadu 33.00 10.00 5 \n32 Telangana 24.11 16.70 6 \n33 Tripura 21.05 11.77 4 \n34 Uttar Pradesh 181.99 129.49 30 \n35 Uttarakhand 23.78 20.59 5 \n36 West Bengal 26.77 8.03 1 \n  Total 2244.52 915.83 267 \n \n\nPage 4 of 5 \n \nANNEXURE REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PART S (a) AND (b)  OF LOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 426 ANSWERED ON 30.11.2021 REGARDING \n“SPORTS INFRASTRUCTURE ” ASKED BY SHRI RAJIV PRATAP RUDY , \nHON’BLE MEMBER OF LOK SABHA \n \nANNEXURE – II \n \nDiscipline-wise coaches under SAI \n \nS. No. Discipline No. of coaches \n1 Archery 47 \n2 Athletics 114 \n3 Badminton 36 \n4 Basketball 28 \n5 Boxing 83 \n6 Cricket 11 \n7 Cycling 22 \n8 Fencing 20 \n9 Football 62 \n10 Gymnastics 44 \n11 Handball 23 \n12 Hockey 59 \n13 Judo 39 \n14 Karate 6 \n15 Kayaking & Canoeing 10 \n16 Kho-Kho & Kabaddi 30 \n17 Lawn Tennis 10 \n18 Rowing 6 \n19 Sailing 1 \n20 Sepak Takraw 6 \n21 Shooting 7 \n22 Softball 3 \n23 Swimming 26 \n24 Table Tennis 22 \n25 Taekwondo 28 \n26 Volleyball 48 \n27 Weightlifting 38 \n28 Wrestling 69 \n29 Wushu 20 \n Total 918 \n \n\nPage 5 of 5 \n \nANNEXURE REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PART S (a) AND (b)  OF LOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 426 ANSWERED ON 30.11.2021 REGARDING \n“SPORTS INFRASTRUCTURE ” ASKED BY SHRI RAJIV PRATAP  RUDY, \nHON’BLE MEMBER OF LOK SABHA \n \nANNEXURE – III \n \nState-wise coaches under SAI \n \nS. No. State/UT No. of coaches \n1 Andhra Pradesh 14 \n2 Andaman and Nicobar Islands 4 \n3 Arunachal Pradesh 10 \n4 Assam 49 \n5 Bihar 5 \n6 Chandigarh 6 \n7 Chhattisgarh 4 \n8 Delhi 108 \n9 Goa 6 \n10 Gujarat 21 \n11 Himachal Pradesh 16 \n12 Haryana 87 \n13 Jammu & Kashmir 8 \n14 Jharkhand 10 \n15 Karnataka 55 \n16 Kerala 51 \n17 Lakshadweep 3 \n18 Madhya Pradesh 47 \n19 Maharashtra 43 \n20 Manipur 49 \n21 Meghalaya 4 \n22 Mizoram 6 \n23 Nagaland 3 \n24 Odisha 20 \n25 Puducherry 10 \n26 Punjab 93 \n27 Rajasthan 17 \n28 Sikkim 2 \n29 Tamil Nadu 17 \n30 Telangana 20 \n31 Tripura 6 \n32 Uttar Pradesh 49 \n33 Uttarakhand 10 \n34 West Bengal 65 \n Total 918 \n \n**** ",
    "ls|LS17_S7_U_427": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF HOME AFFAIRS \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 427 \n \nTO BE ANSWERED ON THE 30th NOVEMBER, 2021/AGRAHAYANA 9,1943 (SAKA) \n \nCASTE-BASED CENSUS \n \n427.  SHRIMATI KANIMOZHI KARUNANIDHI: \nSHRI MALOOK NAGAR: \n \nWill the Minister of HOME AFFAIRS be pleased to state:  \n \n(a) whether the Government has considered  conducting a caste -based census in \n2021-22; \n \n(b) if so, the details thereof and if not, the reasons therefor; \n \n(c) whether the Government has formulated any  scheme/policy for caste -based \ncensus in various States including Uttar Pradesh; \n \n(d) if so, the details thereof and if not, the reasons therefor; \n \n(e) the number of caste communities identified across  the country, State -wise; \nand \n \n(f) whether the Governmen t proposes to include  more castes/communities in the \nScheduled Castes category, if so, the details thereof? \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF HOME AFFAIRS \n(SHRI NITYANAND RAI) \n(a) to (f): The castes and tribes which are specifically notified as  Scheduled \nCastes (SCs) and Scheduled Tribes (STs) as per the Constitution (Scheduled \nCastes)  Order  1950  and  the   Constitution  (Scheduled Tribes)  Order,   1950, as \n \n\n-2- \nLS.USQ.NO.427 FOR 30.11.2021 \namended from time to time, are enumerated in decadal Census. The Government \nof India has not enumerated caste wise population other than SCs and STs in \nCensus since independence.  The Census Schedule is designed in consultation \nwith various stakeholders including Central Ministries.  The intent of th e \nGovernment for conducting Census 2021 was notified in the Gazette of India on \n28th March, 2019.  However,  due to outbreak of Covid -19, the Census activities \nhave been postponed.  \n \n***** \n            \n  ",
    "ls|LS17_S7_U_428": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF HOME AFFAIRS \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 428 \n \nTO BE ANSWERED ON THE 30th NOVEMBER, 2021/AGRAHAYANA 9,1943 (SAKA) \n \nCASES OF CYBER CRIME RELATED TO FINANCIAL FRAUD \n \n428. SHRI M. BADRUDDIN AJMAL: \n \nWill the Minister of HOME AFFAIRS be pleased to state:  \n \n(a) whether a sharp increase has been reported  in cyber crime related to \nfinancial frauds in the country; \n \n(b) if so, the number of such cases reported during  the last five years in the \ncountry, year-wise along with the type of frauds; \n \n(c) the percentage of such cases and the action taken against the culprits; \n \n(d) the measures and mechanism being adopted by  the Government to curb \nsuch cases; and  \n \n(e) the details and number of fake and fraud cal l centres which were busted \nduring the said period? \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF HOME AFFAIRS \n(SHRI AJAY KUMAR MISHRA) \n(a) to (e): With the enhanced use of internet, the number of cyber crimes \nincluding financial frauds is also increasing. The National Crime Records \nBureau (NCRB) compiles and publishes statistical data on crimes in its \npublication “Crime in India”. The latest published report is for the year 2020. As \nper  data  published  by  NCRB,   the  details  of cases registered,  action taken  \n \n\n--2-- \nLS.US.Q.NO. 428 FOR 30.11.2021 \nagainst the culprits etc., under cyber crimes, States/UTs wise and category \nwise during 2016-2020 are at Annexure. \n„Police‟ and „Public Order* are State subjects as per the Seventh Schedule \nof the Constitution of India. States/UTs are primarily responsible  for the \nprevention, detection, investigation and prosecution of crimes through their \nLaw Enforcement Agencies (LEAs). The LEAs take legal action as per \nprovisions of law against the offenders.  \nTo  strengthen  the  mechanism  to  deal  with  cyber  crimes  in  a \ncomprehensive  and  coordinated  manner,  the  Central  Government  has \ntaken  steps  for  spreading  awareness  about  cyber  crimes;  issuance  of  \nalerts/ advisories; capacity building /training of   law  enforcement personnel/ \nprosecutors/judicial officers; improving cyber forensic facilities; etc. Ministry of \nHome Affairs has established Indian Cyber Crime Coordination Centre (I4C) to \nprovide a framework and eco -system for LEAs to deal  with the cyber crimes in \na comprehensive and coordinated manner.  \nThe Government has launched the National Cyber Crime Reporting Portal \n(www.cybercrime.gov.in), to enable public to report incidents pertaining to all \ntypes of cyber crimes, with a special focus on cyber crimes against women and \nchildren. Cyber  crime  incidents  reported  on  this  portal, their conversion into  \n\n--3-- \nLS.US.Q.NO. 428 FOR 30.11.2021 \nFIRs and subsequent action thereon are handled by the State/UT law \nenforcement agency concerne d as per the provisions of the law. Citizen \nFinancial Cyber Fraud Reporting and Management System has been launched \nfor immediate reporting of financial frauds and to stop siphoning off funds by \nthe fraudsters.  A toll -free number 155260 has been operationa lized to get \nassistance in lodging online cyber complaints. \nThe details and number of fake and fraud call centres are not maintained \ncentrally. \n***** \n  \n\n \nAnnexure \nL.S.USQ.NO.428 FOR 30.11.2021 \nCrime Head-wise Cases Registered (CR), Cases Chargesheeted (CCS), Cases Convicted (CON), Persons Arrested (PAR), Persons Chargesheeted (PCS) and Persons Convicted (PCV) \nunder Cyber Crimes during 2016-2017 \nSL Crime Head 2016 2017 \nCR CCS CON PAR PCS PCV CR CCS CON PAR PCS PCV \n1 Tampering computer source documents 78 32 1 66 43 1 233 54 2 99 72 2 \n2 Computer Related Offences 6818 2018 134 4674 2579 172 10108 2091 50 4169 3319 59 \n3 Cyber Terrorism 12 6 0 7 7 0 13 1 0 11 1 0 \n4 Publication/transmission of obscene / sexually explicit act in electronic form  957 409 10 829 484 12 1768 642 18 1443 1087 25 \n5 Interception or Monitoring or decryption of Information 0 0 0 1 0 0 4 1 1 2 1 1 \n6 Un-authorized access/attempt to  access to protected computer system  0 3 0 3 3 0 2 1 0 1 1 0 \n7 Abetment to Commit Offences - - - - - - 0 0 0 0 0 0 \n8 Attempt to Commit Offences - - - - - - 4 0 0 0 0 0 \n9 Other Sections of IT Act 748 242 14 384 298 17 1503 379 44 658 522 37 \nA Total Offences under I.T. Act 8613 2710 159 5964 3414 202 13635 3169 115 6383 5003 124 \n10 Abetment of Suicide (Online) - - - - - - 0 0 0 0 0 0 \n11 Cyber Stalking/Bullying of Women/Children - - - - - - 542 233 6 397 296 6 \n12 Data theft 86 22 0 34 26 0 307 36 0 155 37 0 \n13 Fraud - - - - - - 3466 614 9 1971 1027 11 \n14 Cheating 2329 355 4 853 569 6 1896 301 2 806 621 2 \n15 Forgery 81 16 0 64 37 0 99 24 0 180 75 0 \n16 Defamation/Morphing - - - - - - 12 4 0 17 17 0 \n17 Fake Profile - - - - - - 86 31 0 90 52 0 \n18 Counterfeiting 10 12 0 17 19 0 1 1 0 9 1 0 \n19 Cyber Blackmailing/Threatening - - - - - - 311 103 2 214 127 2 \n20 Fake News on Social Media - - - - - - 170 51 1 115 85 1 \n21 Other Offences 1012 454 9 817 620 15 1086 518 12 1037 768 11 \nB Total Offences under IPC 3518 859 13 1785 1271 21 7976 1916 32 4991 3106 33 \n22 Gambling Act (Online Gambling) - - - - - - 45 28 0 102 101 0 \n23 Lotteries Act (Online Lotteries) - - - - - - 11 0 0 1 0 0 \n24 Copy Right Act 181 136 29 237 212 31 89 57 4 103 82 4 \n25 Trade Marks Act 2 0 0 0 0 0 11 1 0 5 1 0 \n26 Other SLL Crimes 3 7 0 4 16 0 29 9 1 16 13 1 \nC Total Offences under SLL(Special and Local Laws) 186 143 29 241 228 31 185 95 5 227 197 5 \nD Total Cyber Crimes 12317 3712 201 7990 4913 254 21796 5180 152 11601 8306 162 \nSource: Crime in India             \n\n \n \nAnnexure \nL.S.USQ.NO.428 FOR 30.11.2021 \nCrime Head-wise Cases Registered (CR), Cases Chargesheeted (CCS), Cases Convicted (CON), Persons Arrested (PAR), Persons Chargesheeted (PCS) and Persons Convicted (PCV) under Cyber Crimes \nduring 2018-20