    "ls|LS17_S7_U_429": " \n \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF FISHERIES, ANIMAL HUSBANDRY AND DAIRYING \nDEPARTMENT OF FISHERIES \n \nLOK SABHA \n \nUNSTARRED QUESTION No. 429 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 30TH NOVEMBER, 2021 \n \n \nImplementation of Pradhan Mantri Matsya Sampada Yojana (PMMSY) \n \n \n429. DR. HEENA GAVIT: \nSHRI UNMESH BHAIYYASAHEB PATIL: \n DR. SUJAY RADHAKRISHNA VIKHE PATIL: \n  SHRI DHAIRYASHEEL SAMBHAJIRAO MANE: \n  DR. SHRIKANT EKNATH SHINDE: \n \n \n        Will the Minister of FISHERIES, ANIMAL HUSBANDRY AND DAIRYING \nमत्स्यपालन, पशुपालन और डेयरी मंत्री sbe pleased to state:  \n \n(a) the details of increase in fish production after the implementation of the Pradhan Mantri  \nMatsya SampadaYojana (PMKSY) in line with the objectives of the scheme, State-wise; \n \n(b) the details of employment generated by way of implementation of the scheme as on date, \nState-wise; \n \n(c) the number of fishermen and fish farmers whose incomes have doubled in line with the \nscheme objectives and the details thereof, State-wise;  \n \n(d) the details of potential growth clusters identified for deve lopment under the scheme, \nState-wise, and the criteria employed for such identification; \n \n(e) the details of steps taken to create robust fisheries management and regulatory framework \nin line with the scheme objectives; and \n \n(f) the monitoring mechanism to keep a check on whether the ambitious goals of the scheme \nare being met or not? \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF FISHERIES, ANIMAL HUSBANDRY AND DAIRYING \n \n(PARSHOTTAM RUPALA) \n(a) to (c): The Department of Fisheries, Ministry of Fisheries, Animal Husbandry and Dairying \nis implementing a flagship scheme namely “Pradhan MantriMatsyaSampadaYojana \n(PMMSY)” with  highest ever estimate investment of ₹20050 crore in fisheries sector for a \nperiod of 5 years with effect from the financial year 2020 -21. The scheme intends to address \ncritical gaps in fish production and productivity, quality, technology, post-harvest infrastructure \nand management, modernization and strengthening of value chain, traceability, establishing a \nrobust fisheries management framework and fishers’ welfare.The activities supported under the \nPMMSY inter-aliaaim towards increase in employment and enhancement  of income of fishers \nand fish farmers in the country. The state-wise details of beneficiaries covered during the year \n2020-21under Pradhan MantriMatsyaSampadaYojana is furnished at Annexure-I.  \n\n \n \n(d):PMMSYalso focuseson creation of f isheries clusters with robust backward and forward \nlinkages.Some of the major activities identified for development under cluster based \napproachin PMMSY are;  (i) brackish -water aquaculture, (ii) mariculture including seaweed \ncultivation, (iii) development of resource specific deep -sea fisheries, (iv) cold water fisheries, \n(iv) development of aquaculture in saline/alkaline areas and (v) integrat ed development of \nreservoirs.  \n(e):The Department of Fisheries, Ministry of Fisheries, Animal Husbandry and Dairying has \ntaken several steps  forrobust fisheries management; they include imposing of uniform fishing \nban in the Indian Exclusive Economic Zone (EEZ) beyond te rritorial waters in East and West \nCoasts for a period of 61 days annually, ban on destructive fishing methods, d iscouraging \njuvenile fishing,  promotion of river and sea ranching, diversification of fisheries activities, use \nof by-catch and juvenile fish e xcluders, turtle exclusion devices, providing need-based support \nto States/UTs for formulation and implementation of fisheries management pla ns,  promotion \nof deep sea fishing and measures to protect the aquatic ecology. \n(f):  The overall implementation of  the PMMSY is monitored through various institutional \narrangements envisagedfor the purpose under the PMMSY at central and state level ssuch as \nCentral Apex Committee (CAC) , Project Monitoring Unit (PMU) , Project Appraisal \nCommittee, Project Monitoring and Evaluation Unit (PMEU)  and State Level Approval & \nMonitoring Committee (SLAMC) . A dedicated portal and  a Management Information System \n(MIS) have been devised for this purpose.  \n***** \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n\n \n \nAnnexure-I \nStatement referred to in reply to the LokSabha Unstarred QuestionNo. 429put in by Dr. \nHeenaGavit, Shri UnmeshBhaiyyasahebPatil , Dr.SujayRadhakrishnaVikhePatil, Shri \nDhairyasheelSambhajirao Mane , Dr. ShrikantEknathShinde Honourable Members of \nParliament for answer on 30 th November, 2021regarding Implementation of Pradhan \nMantriMatsyaSampadaYojana (PMMSY) \n \n \nSl. \nNo. \nState/UT TotalBeneficiaries under PMMSY \n2020-21 \n1 Andhra Pradesh 9614 \n2 Andaman &Nicobar  123 \n3 Arunachal Pradesh 163 \n4 Assam 5662 \n5 Bihar 2809 \n6 Chhattisgarh 10118 \n7 Daman & Diu and Dadra & Nagar Haveli 30 \n8 Delhi  29 \n9 Goa 251 \n10 Gujarat 7516 \n11 Haryana 709 \n12 Himachal Pradesh  954 \n13 Jammu & Kashmir 17941 \n14 Jharkhand 1174 \n15 Karnataka 27636 \n16 Kerala 1010 \n17 Ladakh 60 \n18 Lakshadweep  692 \n19 Madhya Pradesh 16995 \n20 Maharashtra 7580 \n21 Manipur  278 \n22 Meghalaya  947 \n23 Mizoram 6466 \n24 Nagaland 314 \n25 Odisha 14298 \n26 Puducherry 29628 \n27 Punjab 481 \n28 Rajasthan 304 \n29 Sikkim 220 \n30 Tamil Nadu 7748 \n31 Telangana 29680 \n32 Tripura 4797 \n33 Uttar Pradesh  2313 \n34 Uttarakhand 634 \n  Total  209174 \n \n***** ",
    "ls|LS17_S7_U_430": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF HOME AFFAIRS \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 430 \n \nTO BE ANSWERED ON THE 30th NOVEMBER, 2021/AGRAHAYANA 9,1943 (SAKA) \n \nNATURAL CALAMITIES IN UTTARAKHAND \n \n430.  SHRI SANJAY SADASHIVRAO MANDLIK: \nSHRI SUBRAT PATHAK: \nSHRI RAVINDRA KUSHWAHA: \nSHRI BIDYUT BARAN MAHATO: \nSHRI SUDHEER GUPTA: \nSHRI SHRIRANG APPA BARNE: \nSHRI PRATAPRAO JADHAV: \n \nWill the Minister of HOME AFFAIRS be pleased to state:  \n \n(a) whether the Government is aware that  Uttarakhand and other parts of the \ncountry recorded high rainfall in the month of October, 2021; \n \n(b) if so, the details thereof and the reasons for the same; \n \n(c) the details of the total number of casualties of  tourists and locals due of this \nuntimely rain and flood; \n \n(d) whether the Government has sent any team to the rain-ravaged areas of these \nStates to assess the  damage caused due to incessant rain and if so, the  details \nand the findings of the team; \n \n(e) whether the Government has declared any  financial assistance to the kith and \nkin of locals and visiting tourists who lost their life and property in the flood; and \n \n(f) whether the Government has announced any  financial package for the State to \ncounter the damage done due to rain and flood and if so, the details thereof? \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF HOME AFFAIRS \n(SHRI NITYANAND RAI) \n\n--2-- \nLS.US.Q.NO. 430 FOR 30.11.2021 \n(a) to (d): As per information received from India Meteorological Department \n(IMD), interaction between the western disturbance and strong lower level \neasterlies associated over South Madhya Pradesh led to extremely heavy rainfall \nactivity over Uttarakhand and over west Uttar Pradesh on 17 th and 18 th October, \n2021. \nThe State Government of Uttarakhand has reported loss of 72 human lives due to \nabnormal hi gh rainfall between October, 17 and 19, 2021. The Government of \nIndia has constituted an Inter -Ministerial Central Team (IMCT) for on the spot \nassessment of damage due to heavy rains in the month of October, 2021 in the \nstate of Uttarakhand, which visited the State on 22 -24, October,2021. On receipt \nof IMCT report, additional financial assistance is  considered, as per the \nestablished procedure.  \n(e) & (f) : The primary responsibility for disaster management rests with the \nState Governments concerned. The State Governments undertake relief measures \nin the wake of natural disasters from the State Disaster Response Fund (SDRF), \nalready placed at their disposal, in accordance with Government of India’s \napproved items and norms.  Additional financial assistance is provided from \nNational Disaster Response Fund (NDRF), as per laid down procedure in case of \ndisaster of ‘severe nature’, which includes an assessment based on the visit of an  \n\n--3-- \nLS.US.Q.NO. 430 FOR 30.11.2021 \nIMCT. The financial  assistance  from  SDRF/NDRF is provided by way of relief and \nnot for compensation of loss suffered/claimed. Distribution of the relief , as per \nGovernment of India approved items and norms of expenditure from SDRF, to the \naffected people on the ground, is the responsibility of the State Government \nconcerned.  \nThe Government of India has already released, in advance, both the installments \nof Central Share of SDRF for the year 2021-22 amounting to Rs. 17,747.20 crore to \nthe all states, including Rs. 749.60 crore, to the State of Uttarakhand.  \n \n***** \n \n \n \n \n \n \n ",
    "ls|LS17_S7_U_431": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF PANCHAYATI RAJ \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 431 \nANSWERED ON-30.11.2021 \n \nE-GOVERNANCE OF PANCHAYATS \n  \n431. SHRI RAVI KISHAN: \n  \nWill the Minister of PANCHAYATI RAJ be pleased to state: \n \n(a) the extent of implementation of e -governance in the Panchayats across the country, at \npresent;  \n \n(b) whether the Government has provided financial assistance to the States for the purpose and \nif so, the details thereof for the last three years and the current year along with the \nutilisation thereof;  \n \n(c) whether power shortage in rural areas is a major hindrance in the proper working of e -\ngovernance of panchayats;  \n \n(d) if so, the details thereof; and  \n \n(e) the other alternative measures adopted or are likely to be adopted by the Government to \novercome the problem of power shortage in this regard? \n \nANSWER \n  \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE FOR PANCHAYATI RAJ  \n(SHRI KAPIL MORESHWAR PATIL) \n \n(a) Under Digital India Programme, Ministry is implementing e -Panchayat Mission Mode \nProject, in the country to revamp the functioning of Panchayats and to make them more \ntransparent, accountable and effective. Ministry launched eGramSwaraj a simplified work -based \nAccounting application to add resses various aspects of Panchayat functioning viz. planning, \naccounting, budgeting including online payments through eGramSwaraj -PFMS Interface for \nservices delivered.  Currently, 2,58,694 Panchayati Raj Institutions have prepared Panchayat \nDevelopment P lans for Financial Year 2021 -22 and 2,75,252 Panchayati Raj Institutions have \nadopted eGramSwaraj for the purpose of accounting.   Further, 2,53,523 Panchayati Raj \nInstitutions have onboarded eGramSwaraj-PFMS Interface for carrying out online transactions.  \n \n\n(b) No direct financial assistant is provided to States / UTs for implementation of e-governance \nin the Panchayats under e-Panchayat Mission Mode Project.  Ministry of Panchayati Raj (MoPR) \nhas been providing programmatic support for strengthening of Pancha yati Raj Institutions (PRIs) \nand capacity building of Elected Representatives and functionaries of PRIs to improve their \nfunctioning and effectiveness and make them self-reliant. \n(c) to (e) Panchayat being a State subject, providing basic infrastructure fa cilities including \ncomputers, electric power supply etc. is primarily the responsibility of concerned State \nGovernment / UT Administration for proper working of e-governance of panchayats. \n \n*** \n \n ",
    "ls|LS17_S7_U_432": " \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA\n \nMINISTRY OF AGRICULT\nURE AND FARMERS WELF\nARE\n \nDEPARTMENT OF AGRICU\nLTURE AND FARMERS WE\nLFARE\n \n \nLOK SABHA\n \nUNSTARRED\n \nQUESTION NO. \n432\n \nTO BE ANSWERED ON TH\nE \n30\nTH\n \nNOVEMBER\n, 2021\n \n \nMODERNIZATION OF AGR\nICULTURE\n \n \n432\n. \n \n    \nSHRIMATI RAKSHA NIKH\nIL K\nHADSE:\n \n   \n        \n \nSHRI MANOJ KOTAK:\n \n \n  \n \nWill the Minister of AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE \nकृ \u0000ष\n \nएवं\n \n\u0000कसान\n \nक\u0000याण\n \nमं\u0000ी\n \nbe pleased to state:\n \n \n(a) whether the Government proposes and\n \naims to modernize agriculture and empower \nmarginal\n \nfarmers;\n \n \n(b) if so, the details thereof;\n \n \n(c) whether agricultural exports have set a record in\n \nrecent years;\n \n \n(d\n) if so, the details of the agricultural exports\n \nduring the last five years; and\n \n \n(e) the details of the agricultural commodities which\n \nare in demand in the international \nmarket?\n \n \nANSWER\n \n \nMINISTER OF AGRICULT\nURE AND FARMERS WELF\nARE\n \n \nकृ \u0000ष\n \nएवं\n \n\u0000कसान\n \nक\u0000याण\n \nमं\n\u0000ी\n     \n(\nSHRI NARENDRA SINGH \nTOMAR\n)\n \n \n(a)&\n(\nb\n):   \n \nThe Government has taken various steps to\n \nmodernizing agriculture\n \nfor \nsustainable growth in agriculture sector. Some of the interventions in this direction are :\n \n \n \n(\ni\n)\n \n  \nCreation of a\n \nnetwork of 72\n2\n \nKrishi Vig\nyan Kendras (KVKs) for dissemination of \nknowledge and information about modern technology etc.\n \n(\nii\n)\n \n  \nInitiatives under Agriculture Technology Management Agency (ATMA) Scheme like \nExtension Reforms, Mass Media Support to Agricultural Extension, Kisan Call Centr\nes, \nAgri\n-\nClinics and Agri\n-\nBusiness Centres, Exhibitions/ Fairs etc.\n \n(\niii\n)\n \n   \nSub Mission on Agricultural Mechanization (SMAM)\n \n \n(\niv\n)\n \n  \nPromotion of Agricultural Mechanization for In\n-\nSitu Management of Crop Residue in \nthe States of Punjab, Haryana, Uttar Pradesh and NC\nT of Delhi\n \n \n(\nv\n)\n \n  \nNational Agriculture e\n-\nMarket platform (e\n-\nNAM) has been established\n \n \n(\nvi\n)\n \n  \nSoil Health Card (SHC) Scheme\n \n \n(\nvii\n)\n \nPer Drop More Crop (PDMC)\n \n \n(\nviii\n)\n \n \nMission for Integrated Development of Horticulture \n \n\n(\nix\n)\n \n   \nPromotion of 10,000 FPOs.\n \n \n(c)\n&(d)\n \n \nAgriculture exports e\nxcluding allied products\n \n(Marine, Meat etc\n.\n) have set \nrecord in 2020\n-\n21 with\n \n \ntotal agri export of\n \n \nRs. 213513.38 Crore which has been highest \nsince last two decades.\n \nThe export details of principle agriculture commodities in last 5 \nyears is \nannexed\n.\n \n \n \n(e)\n \n \n \nThe top 10 agriculture commodities which are in demand in the international \nmarket are Rice (other than basmati), rice\n-\nbasmati, spices, sugar, cotton, oil\n-\nmeals, castor \noil, fresh fruits, tea and fresh vegetables.\n \n \n \n\nAnnexure\n \n \nIndia's export of principa\nl agri and allied 2016\n-\n17 and 2020\n-\n21\n \n( Value in Rs Crore)\nS. \nNo.\n \nCommodity\n \n2016\n-\n17\n \n2017\n-\n18\n \n2018\n-\n19\n \n2019\n-\n20\n \n2020\n-\n21\n \n1\n \nRICE(OTHER THAN BASMOTI)\n \n16929.88\n23437.23\n21171.17\n14400.32\n35557.03\n2\n \nRICE \n-\nBASMOTI\n \n21512.91\n26870.67\n32804.30\n31026.33\n29847.70\n3\n \nSPICES\n \n19111.25\n20084.91\n23217.77\n25642.04\n29529.39\n4\n \nSUGAR\n \n8659.54\n5225.60\n9523.14\n13981.56\n20668.57\n5\n \nCOTTON RAW INCLD. WASTE\n \n10907.32\n12200.05\n14627.55\n7539.53\n13968.38\n6\n \nOIL MEALS\n \n5410.10\n7043.15\n10557.48\n5861.35\n11688.56\n7\n \nCASTOR OIL\n \n4521.51\n6730.\n00\n6170.12\n6323.84\n6801.99\n8\n \nFRESH FRUITS\n \n4974.21\n4913.28\n5538.15\n5496.38\n5668.75\n9\n \nTEA\n \n4905.64\n5396.65\n5828.34\n5851.11\n5603.50\n10\n \nFRESH VEGETABLES\n \n5790.71\n5297.72\n5679.10\n4617.34\n5388.03\n11\n \nGROUNDNUT\n \n5444.33\n3386.30\n3297.32\n5096.39\n5380.24\n12\n 