    "ls|LS17_S7_U_492": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF MINES \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 492 \nANSWERED ON 01.12.2021 \nMINERAL BLOCKS \n492. SHRI M. BADRUDDIN AJMAL: \nWill the Minister of MINES be pleased to state: \n(a) the details and the number of mine blocks of Iron Ore, minor minerals, Aluminium \nand Copper, Manganese, Bauxite and sand currently located in Assam;  \n(b) the names and the number of mines which are in operational state and those that \nare not operational;  \n(c) the reasons for closing mine specially mineral blocks in Assam;  \n(d) the total number of mine blocks auction during the last five years in the country \nincluding the detail of the revenue Government earned from these mines blocks during \nlast five years; and  \n(e) the measures taken/to be taken by Government to curb illegal mining? \n  \nANSWER \nTHE MINISTER OF MINES, COAL AND PARLIAMENTARY AFFAIRS  \n(SHRI PRALHAD JOSHI) \n(a) to (c): As per information provided by State Government of Assam, at present, there \nare no mineral blocks of iron ore, Aluminium and Copper, Manganese, Bauxite in \nAssam. As per information provided by Indian Bureau of Mines, a subordinate office of \nMinistry of Mines, 7 mineral blocks are in   Assam  of which two are working mines. \nDetails of  major mineral mines in State of  Assam is  provided in Annexure I . However, \nin respect of minor minerals including sand , section 15 of the MMDR Act, 1957, \nempowers the State Governments to make rules for regulating the grant of minor \nmineral concessions.  \n(d): Since the amendment to MMDR Act, 1957, a total of 125 mineral blocks have been \nauctioned so far across 9 States. Out of these 125 blocks, 35 blocks are operational in 3 \nStates. As per information provided by State Government, details of revenue e arned \nfrom these 35  auctioned blocks as auction premium  is provided in Annexure II. \n(e): Section 23C of the MMDR Act, empowers the State Governments to make rules for \npreventing illegal mining, transportation and storage of minerals and for the purposes \nconnected therewith. Accordingly, Govt. of Assam has notified the Assam Mineral \nRegulation and Dealers Rules, 2020 to curb illegal mining in the State. \n \n***** \n  \n\n Annexure I referred to in part (a) to (c) of reply to Unstarred Question No – 492 \nSERIAL \nNO DISTRICT MINERAL \nWorking \nor Non-\nWorking \n  \nNAME OF THE MINE \nAREA \nIN \nHECTS \n1 Karbi \nAnglong LIMESTONE Working  DILLAI PARBAT, BOKAJAN 171.00 \n2 \nDima \nHasao \nLIMESTONE Working  JAMUNA NAGAR LIMESTONE \nMINES, UMRANGSHO 35.00 \n3 LIMESTONE Non-\nWorking \nNEW UMRANGSHO LIMESTONE \nMINES 167.00 \n4 LIMESTONE Non-\nWorking \nNEW UMRANGSHO LIMESTONE \nMINES 33.00 \n5 LIMESTONE Non-\nWorking UCL,18th Kilo, UMRANGSHO 31.00 \n6 LIMESTONE Non-\nWorking UMRANGSHO LIMESTONE MINE 35.00 \n7 LIMESTONE Non-\nWorking UMRANGSHO 417.50 \n  \nAnnexure II  referred to in part (d)   of reply to Unstarred Question No – \n492                                    \nAuction premium collected from the auctioned   mines which are made operational in three \nStates ( As per the information provided by the concerned States)  \n(Amount in Rs Cr.) \nS.No. State No. of \nauctioned \nmines which \nare \noperational \n  \n2017-18 2018-19 2019-20 2020-21 2021-22 \n1 Andhra \nPradesh 1 Not \nAvailable    \n7.52  Not \nAvailable   \nNot \nAvailable  \nNot \nAvailable  \n2 Odisha \n22 \n No \nauctioned \nblock \noperational   \n No \nauctioned \nblock \noperational  \n  \n No \nauctioned \nblock \noperational  \n  \n  \n4,544 \n  \n  \n12,370 \n  \n3 Karnataka 12 3.39 217.4 549.6 1080 1017* \n  \n Figures till Oct 2021 \n* In case of one mine, Figure is up to April 2021 ",
    "ls|LS17_S7_U_493": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF ELECTRONICS AND INFORMATION TECHNOLOGY  \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION No.493 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON: 01.12.2021 \n  \n \nNATIONAL INSTITUTE \n \n \n493.      SHRI KRIPANATH MALLAH:  \n \nWill the Minister of Electronics and Information Technology be pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether the Government has planned to establish the National Institute of Electronics \nand Information Technology Centres in the State of Assam and if so, the details thereof; \n(b) the details of the funds estimated and targets set for the same; \n(c) whether the Government has identified the locations for these centres in the State of \nAssam; and  \n(d) if so, the district -wise details thereof and the time by when the y are likely to be \nestablished? \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE FOR ELECTRONICS AND INFORMATION TECHNOLOGY \n(SHRI RAJEEV CHANDRASEKHAR) \n \n(a) to (d): NIELIT has so far established 07 Centers in the state of Assam. The details of the se \ncenters are as given below: \n \nSl. \nNo. \nNIELIT Centre District Establishment year Budget Outlay  \n(in Rs. Cr.) \n1 Guwahati Kamrup Metropolitan \nDistrict \n1998 4.09 \n2 Jorhat Jorhat 2013 20.67 \n3 Silchar Cachar 2013 5.06 \n4 Kokrajhar Kokrajhar 2014 17.66 \n5 Tezpur Sonitpur 2015 14.69 \n6 Dibrugarh Dibrugarh 2017 17.84 \n7 Majuli Majuli 2019 0.67 \n\n \n \nSo far, approx. 60,000 candidates have been trained/certified by NIELIT in the state of  Assam in \nvarious IT/ITeS training courses on the above mentioned Centres.  \n \n******** \n \n ",
